{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824339574,"sessionID":"ses_f462a5515ffeVXejhZ4OL349Hs","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d80df1001UEdefj3ctCd1su","sessionID":"ses_f462a5515ffeVXejhZ4OL349Hs","messageID":"msg_0b9d807f0001UoWHg79OQiMOGT","type":"tool","id":"call_00_ET_BAgRijcIwMq38ovKwk0W7209","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":250,"path":"/tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt, lines 1-250\n1: ========== PAGE 27 (segment 32, zeq=[26]) ==========\n2: Table of Contents\n3: THE MERGER\n4: Background of the Merger\n5: Background of the Merger.\n6: The Mac-Gray Board of Directors, whom we sometimes to refer to as the Board, and Mac-Gray's management have\n7: continually engaged in a review of Mac-Gray's business plans and other strategic alternatives as part of their\n8: ongoing activities. This process has included evaluating prospects and options pertaining to Mac-Gray's\n9: business, the markets in which it competes, organic initiatives, and possible strategic transactions, such as\n10: mergers, acquisitions and dispositions, in each case with a view towards enhancing value for Mac-Gray's\n11: stockholders.\n12: Certain potential strategic transactions have been discussed from time to time between Mac-Gray and CSC and\n13: other participants in the laundry facilities management industry, including discussions between Mac-Gray and\n14: CSC in the winter of 2011 and into 2012 regarding the possible acquisition of CSC by Mac-Gray. In connection\n15: with Mac-Gray's consideration of a possible acquisition of CSC, which we refer to as the 2011-2012\n16: Discussions, Mac-Gray engaged BofA Merrill Lynch on October 23, 2012. During the fall and winter of 2012, CSC\n17: engaged in a sale process and, ultimately, CSC was purchased by Pamplona in May 2013.\n18: As part of its ongoing review of strategic alternatives, at an in-person meeting of the Board on April 5,\n19: 2013, the Board discussed the sale of CSC to Pamplona as well as industry-wide merger and acquisition activity\n20: and dynamics, and authorized Mac-Gray management to work with BofA Merrill Lynch to explore transactional\n21: opportunities that might be available to Mac-Gray, including opportunities with another strategic party, whom\n22: we refer to as Party A, in the laundry facilities management industry. The Board also determined to further\n23: consider Mac-Gray's strategic alternatives at its meeting scheduled for May 9, 2013.\n24: On April 8, 2013, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch, as instructed by the Board, telephoned a\n25: representative of Party A to discuss generally a possible business combination between Party A and Mac-Gray.\n26: During this call, the representative of Party A indicated that Party A might consider a business combination\n27: with Mac-Gray.\n28: On May 9, 2013, the Mac-Gray Board of Directors held a regularly scheduled in-person meeting. By invitation of\n29: the Board, Stewart G. MacDonald, Jr., our chief executive officer, Michael J. Shea, our executive vice\n30: president and chief financial officer, Neil MacLellan and Phil Emma, executive vice presidents at Mac-Gray,\n31: Linda Serafini, our vice president, general counsel and secretary, and representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch\n32: and Goodwin Procter LLP, outside legal counsel to Mac-Gray, were also in attendance for all or portions of the\n33: meeting. At this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch discussed CSC's sale process and the acquisition environment\n34: generally and provided an overview of strategic alternatives for Mac-Gray, including the continued pursuit of\n35: Mac-Gray's existing business plan, a significant stock repurchase program, acquisition opportunities and the\n36: possibility of a sale process. With respect to a potential sale process, BofA Merrill Lynch discussed the\n37: then-current merger and acquisition environment and Mac-Gray's potential attractiveness as an acquisition\n38: target, including a general overview of potential valuation parameters. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported on\n39: its conversation with the representative of Party A, who had expressed interest in pursuing a possible\n40: business combination with Mac-Gray. Management and BofA Merrill Lynch also reviewed with the Board potential\n41: opportunities if Mac-Gray were to remain independent, including the ability to improve profitability and\n42: EBITDA margins by increasing scale and density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as\n43: the ability to increase revenue through vend increases and the conversion from coin-operated technology to\n44: card-based technology. In considering these alternatives, the Board considered the risks associated with\n45: executing and achieving Mac-Gray's short and long-term business and financial objectives, including Mac-Gray's\n46: ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses, establish\n47: new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically dominated by\n48: smaller local and regional\n49: 27\n50: \n51: ========== PAGE 28 (segment 33, zeq=[27]) ==========\n52: Table of Contents\n53: operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of\n54: market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price.\n55: After a review of these opportunities and risks, the Board engaged in a general discussion concerning whether\n56: it might be advisable to approach and discuss a potential sale transaction with third parties at this time as\n57: a means by which to deliver value to the stockholders of Mac-Gray. The Board discussed the potential risks and\n58: benefits of commencing a process in which parties would be invited to review confidential information and\n59: submit indications of interest with respect to a potential business combination involving Mac-Gray. In\n60: particular, the Board discussed the potential disruptions to Mac-Gray's business, the risk of leaks that might\n61: arise from approaching potential acquirors within the industry and the impact on Mac-Gray's business that\n62: could result from any such leaks, including the potential loss of customers, suppliers and employees resulting\n63: from uncertainty over the future of Mac-Gray. The Board also discussed the potential need to disclose during\n64: such process sensitive, proprietary and confidential information to competitors and potential competitors.\n65: After BofA Merrill Lynch and management left the meeting, a representative of Goodwin Procter provided an\n66: overview of the Board's fiduciary duties and the Board discussed engaging a financial advisor to assist the\n67: Board in considering strategic alternatives, including a sale process. To assist in the administration of the\n68: strategic alternatives review, the Board formed a transaction committee at this meeting, which we refer to as\n69: the Transaction Committee, and appointed Thomas Bullock and Paul Daoust, both independent directors, to serve\n70: as its members. Mr. Daoust was appointed because of his knowledge of and experience with mergers and\n71: acquisitions, including as president, chief executive officer and board member of Salary.com when it was sold\n72: in 2010. Mr. Bullock was appointed because of his knowledge of Mac-Gray and our business, given his tenure on\n73: the Board (since November 2000) and his chairmanship role. Further, both Mr. Daoust and Mr. Bullock indicated\n74: that they would be able to commit sufficient time to undertake the responsibilities associated with membership\n75: on the Transaction Committee. The Transaction Committee was charged with recommending to the Board the\n76: selection and engagement of a financial advisor to assist the Board in seeking and considering strategic\n77: alternatives, including but not limited to a merger, consolidation, business combination, recapitalization,\n78: restructuring, going-private or other strategic transaction or alternative that might be available to Mac-Gray\n79: in lieu of pursuing any of the foregoing. The Board also delegated to the Transaction Committee authority to\n80: take any administrative actions in connection with the Board's exploration of strategic alternatives. The\n81: Board concluded that it would discuss the review of strategic alternatives, including engaging in a potential\n82: sale process, at its next meeting so that the Board would have additional time to consider the risks and\n83: benefits of the various alternatives discussed.\n84: On May 10, 2013, the Transaction Committee and representatives of Goodwin Procter held a meeting by telephone\n85: conference call. The Transaction Committee discussed the process for hiring a financial advisor for a review\n86: of strategic alternatives and determined to invite three nationally recognized investment banks to submit\n87: proposals. The members of the Transaction Committee also discussed what role, if any, members of Mac-Gray\n88: management should have in connection with the Board's consideration of strategic alternatives. In this regard,\n89: the Transaction Committee considered the fact that, in view of the significant stock ownership of Mr.\n90: MacDonald and his family, coupled with the possibility that the strategic alternatives process might result in\n91: third parties who would be interested in partnering with Mr. MacDonald (either through continued employment\n92: and/or contribution of some or all of his existing equity, a so-called equity \"rollover\"), Mr. MacDonald could\n93: have interests that would not be aligned with other Mac-Gray stockholders. As such, the Transaction Committee\n94: determined to recommend to the Board at an upcoming meeting that, as a general matter, Mr. MacDonald should\n95: not be permitted to participate in the Board's evaluation of strategic alternatives or any negotiations with\n96: any interested parties in a sale process. The Transaction Committee noted, however, that Mr. MacDonald would\n97: be invited to provide perspectives to the Board on Mac-Gray's operations and business on an as-requested\n98: basis.\n99: 28\n100: \n101: ========== PAGE 29 (segment 34, zeq=[28]) ==========\n102: Table of Contents\n103: On May 14, 2013, the Transaction Committee and representatives of Goodwin Procter held a meeting by telephone\n104: conference call. The Transaction Committee discussed further the process for hiring a financial advisor for a\n105: review of strategic alternatives. Given that BofA Merrill Lynch was one of the investment banks to be invited\n106: to submit a proposal and that the CSC sale process had ended, the Transaction Committee determined to\n107: terminate BofA Merrill Lynch's engagement letter entered into for purposes of the 2011-2012 Discussions.\n108: On May 15, 2013, Mac-Gray sent a letter to BofA Merrill Lynch terminating their engagement in connection with\n109: the 2011-2012 Discussions.\n110: On May 22, 2013, the Mac-Gray Board of Directors held a regularly scheduled in-person meeting. By invitation\n111: of the Board, Mr. MacDonald, Mr. Shea, Mr. MacLellan, Mr. Emma, Ms. Serafini and representatives of Goodwin\n112: Procter were also present for all or portions of the meeting. At the request of the Board, at this meeting Mr.\n113: MacDonald provided an overview of Mac-Gray's acquisition strategy and recent acquisitions within the industry,\n114: and Mr. MacLellan reviewed Mac-Gray's recently completed acquisitions with the Board. Also at this meeting, in\n115: addition to routine and administrative matters and the approval of Mac-Gray's quarterly common stock dividend,\n116: the Board appointed Mr. Daoust as chairman of the Transaction Committee during an executive session that\n117: excluded all members of management.\n118: Later on May 22, 2013, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch presented to the members of the Transaction\n119: Committee for purposes of a potential engagement in connection with the Board's current review of strategic\n120: alternatives and the possibility of undertaking a sale process.\n121: On May 29, 2013, each of the other two financial advisor candidates presented to the members of the\n122: Transaction Committee for purposes of a potential engagement in connection with the Board's current review of\n123: strategic alternatives and the possibility of undertaking a sale process.\n124: On May 30, 2013, the Board held an in-person meeting. By invitation of the Board, Mr. MacDonald, Mr. Shea, Ms.\n125: Serafini and representatives of Goodwin Procter were also present for all or portions of the meeting. During\n126: the meeting, the members of the Transaction Committee presented their assessment of the relative capabilities,\n127: strengths, and possible weaknesses of the three financial advisor candidates. Mr. Shea also provided his\n128: perspective of each financial advisor candidate's capabilities, strengths, and possible weaknesses. The\n129: Transaction Committee recommended to the Board that Mac-Gray engage BofA Merrill Lynch as Mac-Gray's financial\n130: advisor for the Board's exploration of strategic alternatives, including a potential sale process. The\n131: Transaction Committee's endorsement of BofA Merrill Lynch was based on, among other things, BofA Merrill\n132: Lynch's knowledge of Mac-Gray, the industry generally, the current environment for mergers and acquisitions\n133: and potential strategic acquirors, as well as BofA Merrill Lynch's proposed process and strategy for\n134: maximizing value to Mac-Gray's stockholders should the Board determine that a sale of the company would be in\n135: the best interests of stockholders. Representatives of Goodwin Procter reviewed with the Board the terms of\n136: proposed BofA Merrill Lynch engagement. Based on the recommendation of the Transaction Committee, the Board\n137: unanimously approved the engagement of BofA Merrill Lynch on the terms discussed at this meeting.\n138: Later on May 30, 2013, Mac-Gray held its annual stockholder meeting at which, among other actions, the Mac-\n139: Gray stockholders elected as directors Michael M. Rothenberg and James E. Hyman, candidates nominated by Mac-\n140: Gray's stockholder, Moab Partners, L.P. and certain of its affiliates, which we refer collectively to as Moab.\n141: Mac-Gray and Moab engaged in a proxy contest leading up to the annual meeting and Mr. Rothenberg and Mr. Hyman\n142: were elected in place of Mac-Gray's incumbent nominees, David W. Bryan and Mary Ann Tocio. Edward McCauley's\n143: term expired at the annual meeting, at which time the size of the Board was reduced to seven. Mr. Rothenberg\n144: is a co-founder and the General Partner of Moab and Mr. Hyman is president and chief executive officer of\n145: TestAmerica, Inc., the nation's largest provider of environmental testing services, a role he has held since\n146: 2011.\n147: 29\n148: \n149: ========== PAGE 30 (segment 35, zeq=[29]) ==========\n150: Table of Contents\n151: On May 31, 2013, Mac-Gray entered into an engagement letter with BofA Merrill Lynch with respect to the\n152: Board's undertaking a review of strategic alternatives, including a potential sale process, and in which BofA\n153: Merrill Lynch agreed not to engage as a possible source of new acquisition financing for any potential buyers\n154: in connection with any such sale process without Mac-Gray's prior written consent.\n155: On June 12, 2013, Mr. Rothenberg and Mr. Hyman met individually with Mr. Bullock, Mr. Daoust, Ms. Serafini and\n156: representatives from Goodwin Procter as part of their orientation as new members of the Board. Each was\n157: provided with an overview of the status of the Board's review of strategic alternatives and a potential sale\n158: process. At the meeting with Mr. Rothenberg, the participants discussed that, to the extent Moab might have an\n159: interest in an equity rollover of all or a portion of Moab's shares of Mac-Gray common stock in connection\n160: with any sale transaction that might result from the Board's strategic alternatives process, Mr. Rothenberg\n161: would need to be excluded from the process from the outset. At the time of this meeting, Moab beneficially\n162: owned approximately 9% of Mac-Gray's outstanding common stock.\n163: On June 21, 2013, Party A submitted an unsolicited proposal to the Mac-Gray Board of Directors offering to\n164: purchase Mac-Gray for an all-cash purchase price of $17.00 to $19.00 per share, which we refer to as the Party\n165: A June 21 proposal.\n166: On June 24, 2013, the Mac-Gray Board of Directors held a regularly scheduled meeting. By invitation of the\n167: Board, certain members of management, including Mr. MacDonald, Mr. Shea, Mr. MacLellan, Mr. Emma, Ms. Serafini\n168: and Sheff Halsey, our chief marketing officer and an executive vice president, and representatives from\n169: Goodwin Procter were also present for some or all of the meeting. During this meeting, representatives of\n170: Goodwin Procter reviewed for the Board its fiduciary duties in the context of considering strategic\n171: alternatives that might be available to Mac-Gray, including a potential sale of the company. The Board also\n172: discussed, with input from the members of the Transaction Committee, whether it would be appropriate for each\n173: of Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg to be excluded from the Board's consideration of strategic alternatives in\n174: view of the possibility that Mr. MacDonald and/or Moab could be asked to partner with a potential acquiror\n175: given their respective equity stakes in Mac-Gray. Following this discussion, the Board decided, and Mr.\n176: MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg agreed, that it would be appropriate to exclude Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg\n177: from the Board's evaluation of strategic alternatives and any negotiations with third parties that might take\n178: place. Mr. MacDonald excused himself after such discussion. In furtherance of the conclusion to exclude Mr.\n179: MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg from the Board's evaluation of strategic alternatives, the Board then established\n180: the Special Committee, comprised of the six independent and disinterested members of the Board (namely, Mr.\n181: Bullock, Mr. Daoust, Mr. Hyman, William F. Meagher, Bruce A. Percelay and Alastair G. Robinson), to, among\n182: other things, explore strategic alternatives to enhance value to Mac-Gray's stockholders and to recommend to\n183: the Board the advisability of any such strategic alternative. In connection with this delegation of authority,\n184: the Special Committee was further authorized to (a) consider and evaluate all proposals that might be received\n185: by Mac-Gray in connection with a possible sale or other significant business transaction, through any form of\n186: transaction, including, without limitation, merger, stock purchase, asset purchase, recapitalization,\n187: reorganization, going-private transaction, consolidation, amalgamation or other transaction, (b) participate\n188: in and direct the negotiation of the material terms and conditions of any such transaction, (c) consider any\n189: alternatives to any such transaction, including without limitation, Mac-Gray's continuing to operate as an\n190: independent company and (d) recommend to the Board the advisability of entering into a definitive agreement\n191: with respect to any such transaction. Mr. Bullock was appointed chairman of the Special Committee. It was also\n192: determined that the Transaction Committee would function as a sub-committee of the Special Committee for the\n193: purpose of taking any administrative actions to discharge the duties and responsibilities of the Special\n194: Committee.\n195: 30\n196: \n197: ========== PAGE 31 (segment 36, zeq=[30]) ==========\n198: Table of Contents\n199: Mr. Rothenberg was then excused and the Board meeting proceeded as a meeting of the Special Committee. By\n200: invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma and representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and\n201: Goodwin Procter were present for all or portions of the meeting. Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma presented Mac-Gray's\n202: five-year strategic business plan and projections, including the with acquisitions case and the without\n203: acquisitions case (see \"—Projected Financial Information\" below), which we refer to as the Projections.\n204: Following the management presentation, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch discussed potential strategic\n205: alternatives and the structure and timing of a potential sale process. The Special Committee reviewed Mac-\n206: Gray's short and long-term business strategies and discussed the five-year strategic business plan, including\n207: Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses,\n208: establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically\n209: dominated by smaller local and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-\n210: Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price. The Special\n211: Committee also discussed the risks involved if Mac-Gray did not execute on its strategic business plan,\n212: including the difficulties with increasing the Company's stock price without a significant or transformative\n213: transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring.\n214: The Special Committee, in follow up to the considerations introduced at the May 9th Board meeting, then\n215: further considered whether it might be advisable to approach and discuss a potential sale transaction with\n216: third parties, including Party A, at this time. The Special Committee discussed the potential risks and\n217: benefits of commencing a process in which parties would be invited to review confidential information and\n218: submit indications of interest with respect to a potential business combination involving Mac-Gray, focusing\n219: again on the potential disruptions to Mac-Gray's business, the risk of leaks that might arise from approaching\n220: potential acquirors within the industry and the resulting impact on Mac-Gray's business and the need to\n221: disclose during such process sensitive, proprietary and confidential information to competitors and potential\n222: competitors. The members of the Special Committee also discussed with BofA Merrill Lynch the likelihood that\n223: strategic parties, including CSC and Party A, should be able to pay a higher price for Mac-Gray because of the\n224: benefit of synergies, and noted their collective belief that Party A would likely increase the Party A June 21\n225: proposal in light of such synergies if Party A were given the opportunity to conduct business and legal due\n226: diligence on a potential acquisition of Mac-Gray. The Special Committee then discussed the other strategic\n227: alternatives, including a significant share repurchase program and an extraordinary dividend, and determined\n228: that these options were not likely to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders as compared to execution of\n229: Mac-Gray's strategic business plan or undertaking a sale of the company. In light of the factors discussed at\n230: this meeting, the Special Committee concluded that it would be in the best interests of stockholders to\n231: consider a potential sale of Mac-Gray and to review this alternative as compared to Mac-Gray's continuing as a\n232: stand-alone company and the risks and opportunities associated with execution of Mac-Gray's strategic business\n233: plan.\n234: The Special Committee, with the assistance of BofA Merrill Lynch and management, also discussed the general\n235: and specific universe of potential acquirors who might be contacted, including those parties the Special\n236: Committee believed had the strongest potential to have an interest in engaging in a strategic transaction with\n237: Mac-Gray. In its evaluation of potential likely acquirors, the Special Committee considered such parties'\n238: financial strength, their experience in acquiring companies in Mac-Gray's industry, such parties' perceived\n239: potential interest in Mac-Gray and potential synergies based on such parties' businesses and legal\n240: considerations. The Special Committee and BofA Merrill Lynch also discussed that although potential strategic\n241: buyers should be able to pay a higher purchase price because of synergies, it was important to solicit\n242: interest from financial buyers, including private equity firms, to maximize the competitive dynamics in the\n243: sale process. This perspective was based on the fact that many financial buyers had engaged in transactions\n244: with companies in industries similar to Mac-Gray and could be in a position to recognize value competitive\n245: with potential strategic buyers. Guided by the foregoing principles, the Special Committee determined to\n246: approach a total of\n247: 31\n248: \n249: ========== PAGE 32 (segment 37, zeq=[31]) ==========\n250: Table of Contents\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 251]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt, lines 1-250\n1: ========== PAGE 27 (segment 32, zeq=[26]) ==========\n2: Table of Contents\n3: THE MERGER\n4: Background of the Merger\n5: Background of the Merger.\n6: The Mac-Gray Board of Directors, whom we sometimes to refer to as the Board, and Mac-Gray's management have\n7: continually engaged in a review of Mac-Gray's business plans and other strategic alternatives as part of their\n8: ongoing activities. This process has included evaluating prospects and options pertaining to Mac-Gray's\n9: business, the markets in which it competes, organic initiatives, and possible strategic transactions, such as\n10: mergers, acquisitions and dispositions, in each case with a view towards enhancing value for Mac-Gray's\n11: stockholders.\n12: Certain potential strategic transactions have been discussed from time to time between Mac-Gray and CSC and\n13: other participants in the laundry facilities management industry, including discussions between Mac-Gray and\n14: CSC in the winter of 2011 and into 2012 regarding the possible acquisition of CSC by Mac-Gray. In connection\n15: with Mac-Gray's consideration of a possible acquisition of CSC, which we refer to as the 2011-2012\n16: Discussions, Mac-Gray engaged BofA Merrill Lynch on October 23, 2012. During the fall and winter of 2012, CSC\n17: engaged in a sale process and, ultimately, CSC was purchased by Pamplona in May 2013.\n18: As part of its ongoing review of strategic alternatives, at an in-person meeting of the Board on April 5,\n19: 2013, the Board discussed the sale of CSC to Pamplona as well as industry-wide merger and acquisition activity\n20: and dynamics, and authorized Mac-Gray management to work with BofA Merrill Lynch to explore transactional\n21: opportunities that might be available to Mac-Gray, including opportunities with another strategic party, whom\n22: we refer to as Party A, in the laundry facilities management industry. The Board also determined to further\n23: consider Mac-Gray's strategic alternatives at its meeting scheduled for May 9, 2013.\n24: On April 8, 2013, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch, as instructed by the Board, telephoned a\n25: representative of Party A to discuss generally a possible business combination between Party A and Mac-Gray.\n26: During this call, the representative of Party A indicated that Party A might consider a business combination\n27: with Mac-Gray.\n28: On May 9, 2013, the Mac-Gray Board of Directors held a regularly scheduled in-person meeting. By invitation of\n29: the Board, Stewart G. MacDonald, Jr., our chief executive officer, Michael J. Shea, our executive vice\n30: president and chief financial officer, Neil MacLellan and Phil Emma, executive vice presidents at Mac-Gray,\n31: Linda Serafini, our vice president, general counsel and secretary, and representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch\n32: and Goodwin Procter LLP, outside legal counsel to Mac-Gray, were also in attendance for all or portions of the\n33: meeting. At this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch discussed CSC's sale process and the acquisition environment\n34: generally and provided an overview of strategic alternatives for Mac-Gray, including the continued pursuit of\n35: Mac-Gray's existing business plan, a significant stock repurchase program, acquisition opportunities and the\n36: possibility of a sale process. With respect to a potential sale process, BofA Merrill Lynch discussed the\n37: then-current merger and acquisition environment and Mac-Gray's potential attractiveness as an acquisition\n38: target, including a general overview of potential valuation parameters. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported on\n39: its conversation with the representative of Party A, who had expressed interest in pursuing a possible\n40: business combination with Mac-Gray. Management and BofA Merrill Lynch also reviewed with the Board potential\n41: opportunities if Mac-Gray were to remain independent, including the ability to improve profitability and\n42: EBITDA margins by increasing scale and density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as\n43: the ability to increase revenue through vend increases and the conversion from coin-operated technology to\n44: card-based technology. In considering these alternatives, the Board considered the risks associated with\n45: executing and achieving Mac-Gray's short and long-term business and financial objectives, including Mac-Gray's\n46: ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses, establish\n47: new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically dominated by\n48: smaller local and regional\n49: 27\n50: \n51: ========== PAGE 28 (segment 33, zeq=[27]) ==========\n52: Table of Contents\n53: operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of\n54: market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price.\n55: After a review of these opportunities and risks, the Board engaged in a general discussion concerning whether\n56: it might be advisable to approach and discuss a potential sale transaction with third parties at this time as\n57: a means by which to deliver value to the stockholders of Mac-Gray. The Board discussed the potential risks and\n58: benefits of commencing a process in which parties would be invited to review confidential information and\n59: submit indications of interest with respect to a potential business combination involving Mac-Gray. In\n60: particular, the Board discussed the potential disruptions to Mac-Gray's business, the risk of leaks that might\n61: arise from approaching potential acquirors within the industry and the impact on Mac-Gray's business that\n62: could result from any such leaks, including the potential loss of customers, suppliers and employees resulting\n63: from uncertainty over the future of Mac-Gray. The Board also discussed the potential need to disclose during\n64: such process sensitive, proprietary and confidential information to competitors and potential competitors.\n65: After BofA Merrill Lynch and management left the meeting, a representative of Goodwin Procter provided an\n66: overview of the Board's fiduciary duties and the Board discussed engaging a financial advisor to assist the\n67: Board in considering strategic alternatives, including a sale process. To assist in the administration of the\n68: strategic alternatives review, the Board formed a transaction committee at this meeting, which we refer to as\n69: the Transaction Committee, and appointed Thomas Bullock and Paul Daoust, both independent directors, to serve\n70: as its members. Mr. Daoust was appointed because of his knowledge of and experience with mergers and\n71: acquisitions, including as president, chief executive officer and board member of Salary.com when it was sold\n72: in 2010. Mr. Bullock was appointed because of his knowledge of Mac-Gray and our business, given his tenure on\n73: the Board (since November 2000) and his chairmanship role. Further, both Mr. Daoust and Mr. Bullock indicated\n74: that they would be able to commit sufficient time to undertake the responsibilities associated with membership\n75: on the Transaction Committee. The Transaction Committee was charged with recommending to the Board the\n76: selection and engagement of a financial advisor to assist the Board in seeking and considering strategic\n77: alternatives, including but not limited to a merger, consolidation, business combination, recapitalization,\n78: restructuring, going-private or other strategic transaction or alternative that might be available to Mac-Gray\n79: in lieu of pursuing any of the foregoing. The Board also delegated to the Transaction Committee authority to\n80: take any administrative actions in connection with the Board's exploration of strategic alternatives. The\n81: Board concluded that it would discuss the review of strategic alternatives, including engaging in a potential\n82: sale process, at its next meeting so that the Board would have additional time to consider the risks and\n83: benefits of the various alternatives discussed.\n84: On May 10, 2013, the Transaction Committee and representatives of Goodwin Procter held a meeting by telephone\n85: conference call. The Transaction Committee discussed the process for hiring a financial advisor for a review\n86: of strategic alternatives and determined to invite three nationally recognized investment banks to submit\n87: proposals. The members of the Transaction Committee also discussed what role, if any, members of Mac-Gray\n88: management should have in connection with the Board's consideration of strategic alternatives. In this regard,\n89: the Transaction Committee considered the fact that, in view of the significant stock ownership of Mr.\n90: MacDonald and his family, coupled with the possibility that the strategic alternatives process might result in\n91: third parties who would be interested in partnering with Mr. MacDonald (either through continued employment\n92: and/or contribution of some or all of his existing equity, a so-called equity \"rollover\"), Mr. MacDonald could\n93: have interests that would not be aligned with other Mac-Gray stockholders. As such, the Transaction Committee\n94: determined to recommend to the Board at an upcoming meeting that, as a general matter, Mr. MacDonald should\n95: not be permitted to participate in the Board's evaluation of strategic alternatives or any negotiations with\n96: any interested parties in a sale process. The Transaction Committee noted, however, that Mr. MacDonald would\n97: be invited to provide perspectives to the Board on Mac-Gray's operations and business on an as-requested\n98: basis.\n99: 28\n100: \n101: ========== PAGE 29 (segment 34, zeq=[28]) ==========\n102: Table of Contents\n103: On May 14, 2013, the Transaction Committee and representatives of Goodwin Procter held a meeting by telephone\n104: conference call. The Transaction Committee discussed further the process for hiring a financial advisor for a\n105: review of strategic alternatives. Given that BofA Merrill Lynch was one of the investment banks to be invited\n106: to submit a proposal and that the CSC sale process had ended, the Transaction Committee determined to\n107: terminate BofA Merrill Lynch's engagement letter entered into for purposes of the 2011-2012 Discussions.\n108: On May 15, 2013, Mac-Gray sent a letter to BofA Merrill Lynch terminating their engagement in connection with\n109: the 2011-2012 Discussions.\n110: On May 22, 2013, the Mac-Gray Board of Directors held a regularly scheduled in-person meeting. By invitation\n111: of the Board, Mr. MacDonald, Mr. Shea, Mr. MacLellan, Mr. Emma, Ms. Serafini and representatives of Goodwin\n112: Procter were also present for all or portions of the meeting. At the request of the Board, at this meeting Mr.\n113: MacDonald provided an overview of Mac-Gray's acquisition strategy and recent acquisitions within the industry,\n114: and Mr. MacLellan reviewed Mac-Gray's recently completed acquisitions with the Board. Also at this meeting, in\n115: addition to routine and administrative matters and the approval of Mac-Gray's quarterly common stock dividend,\n116: the Board appointed Mr. Daoust as chairman of the Transaction Committee during an executive session that\n117: excluded all members of management.\n118: Later on May 22, 2013, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch presented to the members of the Transaction\n119: Committee for purposes of a potential engagement in connection with the Board's current review of strategic\n120: alternatives and the possibility of undertaking a sale process.\n121: On May 29, 2013, each of the other two financial advisor candidates presented to the members of the\n122: Transaction Committee for purposes of a potential engagement in connection with the Board's current review of\n123: strategic alternatives and the possibility of undertaking a sale process.\n124: On May 30, 2013, the Board held an in-person meeting. By invitation of the Board, Mr. MacDonald, Mr. Shea, Ms.\n125: Serafini and representatives of Goodwin Procter were also present for all or portions of the meeting. During\n126: the meeting, the members of the Transaction Committee presented their assessment of the relative capabilities,\n127: strengths, and possible weaknesses of the three financial advisor candidates. Mr. Shea also provided his\n128: perspective of each financial advisor candidate's capabilities, strengths, and possible weaknesses. The\n129: Transaction Committee recommended to the Board that Mac-Gray engage BofA Merrill Lynch as Mac-Gray's financial\n130: advisor for the Board's exploration of strategic alternatives, including a potential sale process. The\n131: Transaction Committee's endorsement of BofA Merrill Lynch was based on, among other things, BofA Merrill\n132: Lynch's knowledge of Mac-Gray, the industry generally, the current environment for mergers and acquisitions\n133: and potential strategic acquirors, as well as BofA Merrill Lynch's proposed process and strategy for\n134: maximizing value to Mac-Gray's stockholders should the Board determine that a sale of the company would be in\n135: the best interests of stockholders. Representatives of Goodwin Procter reviewed with the Board the terms of\n136: proposed BofA Merrill Lynch engagement. Based on the recommendation of the Transaction Committee, the Board\n137: unanimously approved the engagement of BofA Merrill Lynch on the terms discussed at this meeting.\n138: Later on May 30, 2013, Mac-Gray held its annual stockholder meeting at which, among other actions, the Mac-\n139: Gray stockholders elected as directors Michael M. Rothenberg and James E. Hyman, candidates nominated by Mac-\n140: Gray's stockholder, Moab Partners, L.P. and certain of its affiliates, which we refer collectively to as Moab.\n141: Mac-Gray and Moab engaged in a proxy contest leading up to the annual meeting and Mr. Rothenberg and Mr. Hyman\n142: were elected in place of Mac-Gray's incumbent nominees, David W. Bryan and Mary Ann Tocio. Edward McCauley's\n143: term expired at the annual meeting, at which time the size of the Board was reduced to seven. Mr. Rothenberg\n144: is a co-founder and the General Partner of Moab and Mr. Hyman is president and chief executive officer of\n145: TestAmerica, Inc., the nation's largest provider of environmental testing services, a role he has held since\n146: 2011.\n147: 29\n148: \n149: ========== PAGE 30 (segment 35, zeq=[29]) ==========\n150: Table of Contents\n151: On May 31, 2013, Mac-Gray entered into an engagement letter with BofA Merrill Lynch with respect to the\n152: Board's undertaking a review of strategic alternatives, including a potential sale process, and in which BofA\n153: Merrill Lynch agreed not to engage as a possible source of new acquisition financing for any potential buyers\n154: in connection with any such sale process without Mac-Gray's prior written consent.\n155: On June 12, 2013, Mr. Rothenberg and Mr. Hyman met individually with Mr. Bullock, Mr. Daoust, Ms. Serafini and\n156: representatives from Goodwin Procter as part of their orientation as new members of the Board. Each was\n157: provided with an overview of the status of the Board's review of strategic alternatives and a potential sale\n158: process. At the meeting with Mr. Rothenberg, the participants discussed that, to the extent Moab might have an\n159: interest in an equity rollover of all or a portion of Moab's shares of Mac-Gray common stock in connection\n160: with any sale transaction that might result from the Board's strategic alternatives process, Mr. Rothenberg\n161: would need to be excluded from the process from the outset. At the time of this meeting, Moab beneficially\n162: owned approximately 9% of Mac-Gray's outstanding common stock.\n163: On June 21, 2013, Party A submitted an unsolicited proposal to the Mac-Gray Board of Directors offering to\n164: purchase Mac-Gray for an all-cash purchase price of $17.00 to $19.00 per share, which we refer to as the Party\n165: A June 21 proposal.\n166: On June 24, 2013, the Mac-Gray Board of Directors held a regularly scheduled meeting. By invitation of the\n167: Board, certain members of management, including Mr. MacDonald, Mr. Shea, Mr. MacLellan, Mr. Emma, Ms. Serafini\n168: and Sheff Halsey, our chief marketing officer and an executive vice president, and representatives from\n169: Goodwin Procter were also present for some or all of the meeting. During this meeting, representatives of\n170: Goodwin Procter reviewed for the Board its fiduciary duties in the context of considering strategic\n171: alternatives that might be available to Mac-Gray, including a potential sale of the company. The Board also\n172: discussed, with input from the members of the Transaction Committee, whether it would be appropriate for each\n173: of Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg to be excluded from the Board's consideration of strategic alternatives in\n174: view of the possibility that Mr. MacDonald and/or Moab could be asked to partner with a potential acquiror\n175: given their respective equity stakes in Mac-Gray. Following this discussion, the Board decided, and Mr.\n176: MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg agreed, that it would be appropriate to exclude Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg\n177: from the Board's evaluation of strategic alternatives and any negotiations with third parties that might take\n178: place. Mr. MacDonald excused himself after such discussion. In furtherance of the conclusion to exclude Mr.\n179: MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg from the Board's evaluation of strategic alternatives, the Board then established\n180: the Special Committee, comprised of the six independent and disinterested members of the Board (namely, Mr.\n181: Bullock, Mr. Daoust, Mr. Hyman, William F. Meagher, Bruce A. Percelay and Alastair G. Robinson), to, among\n182: other things, explore strategic alternatives to enhance value to Mac-Gray's stockholders and to recommend to\n183: the Board the advisability of any such strategic alternative. In connection with this delegation of authority,\n184: the Special Committee was further authorized to (a) consider and evaluate all proposals that might be received\n185: by Mac-Gray in connection with a possible sale or other significant business transaction, through any form of\n186: transaction, including, without limitation, merger, stock purchase, asset purchase, recapitalization,\n187: reorganization, going-private transaction, consolidation, amalgamation or other transaction, (b) participate\n188: in and direct the negotiation of the material terms and conditions of any such transaction, (c) consider any\n189: alternatives to any such transaction, including without limitation, Mac-Gray's continuing to operate as an\n190: independent company and (d) recommend to the Board the advisability of entering into a definitive agreement\n191: with respect to any such transaction. Mr. Bullock was appointed chairman of the Special Committee. It was also\n192: determined that the Transaction Committee would function as a sub-committee of the Special Committee for the\n193: purpose of taking any administrative actions to discharge the duties and responsibilities of the Special\n194: Committee.\n195: 30\n196: \n197: ========== PAGE 31 (segment 36, zeq=[30]) ==========\n198: Table of Contents\n199: Mr. Rothenberg was then excused and the Board meeting proceeded as a meeting of the Special Committee. By\n200: invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma and representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and\n201: Goodwin Procter were present for all or portions of the meeting. Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma presented Mac-Gray's\n202: five-year strategic business plan and projections, including the with acquisitions case and the without\n203: acquisitions case (see \"—Projected Financial Information\" below), which we refer to as the Projections.\n204: Following the management presentation, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch discussed potential strategic\n205: alternatives and the structure and timing of a potential sale process. The Special Committee reviewed Mac-\n206: Gray's short and long-term business strategies and discussed the five-year strategic business plan, including\n207: Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses,\n208: establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically\n209: dominated by smaller local and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-\n210: Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price. The Special\n211: Committee also discussed the risks involved if Mac-Gray did not execute on its strategic business plan,\n212: including the difficulties with increasing the Company's stock price without a significant or transformative\n213: transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring.\n214: The Special Committee, in follow up to the considerations introduced at the May 9th Board meeting, then\n215: further considered whether it might be advisable to approach and discuss a potential sale transaction with\n216: third parties, including Party A, at this time. The Special Committee discussed the potential risks and\n217: benefits of commencing a process in which parties would be invited to review confidential information and\n218: submit indications of interest with respect to a potential business combination involving Mac-Gray, focusing\n219: again on the potential disruptions to Mac-Gray's business, the risk of leaks that might arise from approaching\n220: potential acquirors within the industry and the resulting impact on Mac-Gray's business and the need to\n221: disclose during such process sensitive, proprietary and confidential information to competitors and potential\n222: competitors. The members of the Special Committee also discussed with BofA Merrill Lynch the likelihood that\n223: strategic parties, including CSC and Party A, should be able to pay a higher price for Mac-Gray because of the\n224: benefit of synergies, and noted their collective belief that Party A would likely increase the Party A June 21\n225: proposal in light of such synergies if Party A were given the opportunity to conduct business and legal due\n226: diligence on a potential acquisition of Mac-Gray. The Special Committee then discussed the other strategic\n227: alternatives, including a significant share repurchase program and an extraordinary dividend, and determined\n228: that these options were not likely to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders as compared to execution of\n229: Mac-Gray's strategic business plan or undertaking a sale of the company. In light of the factors discussed at\n230: this meeting, the Special Committee concluded that it would be in the best interests of stockholders to\n231: consider a potential sale of Mac-Gray and to review this alternative as compared to Mac-Gray's continuing as a\n232: stand-alone company and the risks and opportunities associated with execution of Mac-Gray's strategic business\n233: plan.\n234: The Special Committee, with the assistance of BofA Merrill Lynch and management, also discussed the general\n235: and specific universe of potential acquirors who might be contacted, including those parties the Special\n236: Committee believed had the strongest potential to have an interest in engaging in a strategic transaction with\n237: Mac-Gray. In its evaluation of potential likely acquirors, the Special Committee considered such parties'\n238: financial strength, their experience in acquiring companies in Mac-Gray's industry, such parties' perceived\n239: potential interest in Mac-Gray and potential synergies based on such parties' businesses and legal\n240: considerations. The Special Committee and BofA Merrill Lynch also discussed that although potential strategic\n241: buyers should be able to pay a higher purchase price because of synergies, it was important to solicit\n242: interest from financial buyers, including private equity firms, to maximize the competitive dynamics in the\n243: sale process. This perspective was based on the fact that many financial buyers had engaged in transactions\n244: with companies in industries similar to Mac-Gray and could be in a position to recognize value competitive\n245: with potential strategic buyers. Guided by the foregoing principles, the Special Committee determined to\n246: approach a total of\n247: 31\n248: \n249: ========== PAGE 32 (segment 37, zeq=[31]) ==========\n250: Table of Contents\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 251]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 250, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824339441,"end":1789824339574}}}}
