{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824341608,"sessionID":"ses_f462a5515ffeVXejhZ4OL349Hs","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d815be001feZNqIxyi52HHw","sessionID":"ses_f462a5515ffeVXejhZ4OL349Hs","messageID":"msg_0b9d80e80001O661gwBIVTJJBl","type":"tool","id":"call_00_ET_ozAZ8vutbSpS35aIKdU85303","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":260,"offset":251,"path":"/tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt, lines 251-510\n251: 50 parties, including CSC and Pamplona, who together we refer to as CSC/Pamplona, and Party A, to solicit\n252: potential interest in a transaction with Mac-Gray, 15 of which were potential strategic buyers and 35 of which\n253: were potential financial buyers, including private equity firms.\n254: During the next several weeks, in accordance with the directives of the Special Committee, BofA Merrill Lynch\n255: contacted the strategic and financial parties identified at the June 24th Special Committee meeting. Over the\n256: next two months a total of 20 potential bidders, including two strategic bidders (Party A and CSC/Pamplona)\n257: and 18 financial bidders (including Party B and Party C), entered into confidentiality agreements with Mac-\n258: Gray on a form provided by Mac-Gray, the terms of which were individually negotiated with potential bidders,\n259: but did not vary materially from the form provided. The confidentiality agreements contained customary non-\n260: disclosure provisions and a customary standstill provision that either expired or permitted the potential\n261: bidder to make confidential proposals to Mac-Gray (a so-called \"sunset\" provision) upon announcement of a\n262: business combination between Mac-Gray and a third party, and, in some instances upon announcement of a change\n263: in control. Parties that had entered into confidentiality and standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were\n264: furnished an informational package for the purpose of providing potential bidders with data to formulate a\n265: preliminary indication of interest, which they were instructed to submit by July 23, 2013. Parties that had\n266: entered into confidentiality and standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were furnished an informational package\n267: for the purpose of providing potential bidders with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest,\n268: which they were instructed to submit by July 23, 2013. The letter requested that the proposals address, among\n269: other things, valuation, sources and structures of financing, material conditions, including required\n270: approvals, and focus areas for due diligence.\n271: On June 28, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee participated in a telephone conference call with\n272: representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter. BofA Merrill Lynch summarized the contacts they had\n273: had with certain of the potential bidders. In particular, BofA Merrill Lynch reported that they had spoken\n274: with representatives of Party A, as instructed by the Special Committee, who had expressed the view that there\n275: would be considerable synergies if Party A were to acquire Mac-Gray and that Party A would be speaking with\n276: certain financing sources with respect to funding such a transaction. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported that\n277: they had spoken with representatives of Pamplona, as instructed by the Special Committee, who expressed\n278: interest in a transaction, including an acquisition of Mac-Gray by its portfolio company, CSC. Pamplona\n279: emphasized that business and legal due diligence would be minimal given its familiarity with Mac-Gray and the\n280: industry generally.\n281: Later on June 28, 2013, Party B, entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was\n282: provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party B with data to formulate a\n283: preliminary indication of interest.\n284: On June 30, 2013, Party C entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was\n285: provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party C with data to formulate a\n286: preliminary indication of interest.\n287: On July 3, 2013, Mr. Rothenberg called a representative of Goodwin Procter to indicate that he had spoken with\n288: representatives of Pamplona about Moab's desire to maintain the flexibility to consider a possible equity\n289: rollover if Pamplona were to participate in a business combination with Mac-Gray. Mr. Rothenberg also informed\n290: Goodwin Procter that Pamplona would be requesting that any confidentiality agreement CSC/Pamplona execute with\n291: Mac-Gray permit CSC and Pamplona to discuss their bid and evaluation material with Moab, with Moab agreeing to\n292: exclusively engage with CSC/Pamplona.\n293: On July 6, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting for the purpose of receiving an update on the\n294: ongoing sale process. By invitation of the Special Committee, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and\n295: Goodwin Procter were also present throughout the meeting. The Special Committee discussed whether Moab should\n296: be permitted to partner exclusively with CSC/Pamplona. Because the\n297: 32\n298: \n299: ========== PAGE 33 (segment 38, zeq=[32]) ==========\n300: Table of Contents\n301: objective of the sale process was to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders, the Special Committee decided\n302: to deny the exclusivity request at this stage of the process so that the potential equity rollover of Moab's\n303: shares could be available as a source of capital for other potential bidders. The Special Committee instructed\n304: BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter to communicate to CSC/Pamplona and Mr. Rothenberg, respectively, that\n305: it was premature for Mac-Gray to permit Moab to engage in equity rollover discussions with any party,\n306: including CSC/Pamplona, but that such discussions would be permitted at a later stage of the sale process.\n307: On July 11, 2013, CSC/Pamplona entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and were\n308: provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing CSC/Pamplona with data to formulate a\n309: preliminary indication of interest.\n310: Between July 11, 2013 and July 23, 2013, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona engaged in a preliminary review of\n311: Mac-Gray and the potential acquisition opportunity. Also during this time, outside counsel to Party A and\n312: representatives of Goodwin Procter negotiated the terms of a confidentiality and standstill agreement, but did\n313: not come to agreement on the terms of the customer and employee non-solicitation provisions.\n314: On July 23, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of\n315: $18.50 per share.\n316: On July 24, 2013, Party B submitted a preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of\n317: $17.00 to $18.00 per share. Also on July 24, 2013, representatives from Party C called BofA Merrill Lynch and\n318: presented an oral preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $15.00 to $17.00 per\n319: share.\n320: On July 25, 2013, the Board held a regularly scheduled in-person meeting. By invitation of the Board, Mr. Shea\n321: and representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also present for all or portions of this\n322: meeting. Prior to the start of the meeting, because the Board expected to discuss matters related to the sale\n323: process, and in light of Moab's continuing interest in a potential equity rollover in any sale transaction,\n324: Mr. Rothenberg recused himself from the meeting and the meeting proceeded as a meeting of the Special\n325: Committee. At this meeting, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed the preliminary indications of\n326: interest received from Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona as well as the Party A June 21 proposal, in each case\n327: from a financial point of view. The Board reviewed management's five-year strategic business plan and\n328: Projections. During this meeting, representatives of Goodwin Procter reviewed for the Special Committee its\n329: fiduciary duties in the context of considering a potential sale. The Special Committee then discussed the\n330: potential conflict of interest arising from Moab's continuing desire to consider an equity rollover and\n331: decided that Mr. Rothenberg should be asked to recuse himself from participating in Board meetings for the\n332: duration of the sale process so he would not be privy to confidential information about Mac-Gray and our\n333: business.\n334: The Special Committee discussed that the preliminary indications of interest were lower than expected and\n335: suggested that additional due diligence, including with regard to the potential synergies, should result in\n336: improved offers by the strategic parties. The Special Committee then discussed the highly competitive nature\n337: of Mac-Gray's industry and expressed concern over permitting access to and the potential misuse of sensitive\n338: and competitive information, including customer terms and locations, especially by strategic bidders.\n339: Accordingly, the Special Committee concluded that it would be advisable to stage the disclosure of such\n340: information to each of the bidders to the extent that a bidder's indication of interest and other actions\n341: demonstrated its seriousness in acquiring Mac-Gray on terms viewed favorably by the Special Committee. Based\n342: on this discussion, BofA Merrill Lynch discussed with the Special Committee a proposed process for the second\n343: stage of the on-going sale process. As part of this review, BofA Merrill Lynch recommended that management\n344: meetings be arranged between members of Mac-Gray management (other than Mr. MacDonald) and each of Party A (if\n345: Party A\n346: 33\n347: \n348: ========== PAGE 34 (segment 39, zeq=[33]) ==========\n349: Table of Contents\n350: agreed to enter into a confidentiality agreement), Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona to provide these parties\n351: with access to high level information concerning Mac-Gray's technology, pipeline, management team, regional\n352: operations, contracts, operations and growth initiatives, and in the case of CSC/Pamplona and Party A,\n353: synergies, and to then request that each of these parties submit a revised indication of interest. After\n354: discussion and in an effort to drive offers with increased value, the Special Committee authorized and\n355: instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to proceed with the staged disclosure as discussed at this meeting and to reach\n356: out to each of the four interested bidders and arrange management meetings.\n357: Later on July 25, 2013, Party C submitted a written indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of\n358: $16.00 to $16.50 per share.\n359: Discussions with Party A resumed on July 27, 2103 and Party A and the Transaction Committee agreed to the\n360: terms of the customer and employee non-solicitation provisions. On August 5, 2013, Party A entered into a\n361: confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the\n362: purpose of providing Party A with data to potentially improve the terms of the Party A June 21 proposal.\n363: On August 6, 2013, representatives of Party B attended a management presentation at the Boston office of\n364: Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and a member of the\n365: Transaction Committee.\n366: On August 8, 2013, representatives of Party C attended a management presentation at the Boston office of\n367: Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and a member of the\n368: Transaction Committee.\n369: On August 12, 2013, representatives of CSC and Pamplona attended a management presentation at the Boston\n370: office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and the\n371: two members of the Transaction Committee.\n372: On August 14, 2013, representatives of Party A attended a management presentation at the Boston office of\n373: Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and the two members\n374: of the Transaction Committee.\n375: On August 15, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting for the purpose of reviewing the on-going\n376: strategic alternatives process. By invitation of the Special Committee, representatives from BofA Merrill\n377: Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also in attendance. BofA Merrill Lynch updated the Special Committee as to the\n378: current status of the strategic alternatives process, including the due diligence process and the management\n379: presentations with Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. After discussion and as discussed at the July\n380: 25th Special Committee meeting, the Special Committee authorized and instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to send a\n381: letter to each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona once all four of the bidders' due diligence was\n382: substantially complete requesting that, in light of the results of the respective party's due diligence, such\n383: party submit a revised written proposal in order to continue in the process, including to engage in more in-\n384: depth and confirmatory due diligence of Mac-Gray.\n385: Between August 6, 2013 and August 27, 2013, the four bidders conducted business and legal due diligence.\n386: During this time, Party B and Party C, in light of the fact that they were financial bidders, were given broad\n387: access to an electronic data site, and Party A and CSC/Pamplona, in light of the fact that they were strategic\n388: bidders, were given somewhat limited access to this electronic data site, with access to customer data,\n389: including customer locations, being reserved until later in the process as part of the staging discussed at\n390: the July 25th Special Committee meeting. During this time, both Party A and CSC/Pamplona participated in\n391: discussions with members of Mac-Gray management around synergies.\n392: 34\n393: \n394: ========== PAGE 35 (segment 40, zeq=[34]) ==========\n395: Table of Contents\n396: On August 27, 2013, BofA Merrill Lynch, as instructed by the Special Committee, sent a letter to each of Party\n397: A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona instructing such bidders to submit revised written proposals no later\n398: than September 9, 2013 if such bidders wished to proceed further in the process to pursue an in-depth\n399: evaluation of Mac-Gray with the objective of making a revised offer. The letter requested that the revised\n400: proposals address, among other things, valuation, sources and structures of financing, regulatory approvals\n401: and other post-signing covenants and focus areas for due diligence.\n402: Also on August 27, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and\n403: representatives of Party B participated in a telephone conference call for Party B to conduct follow-up and\n404: confirmatory due diligence.\n405: On August 29, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and\n406: representatives of Party C participated in an in-person session for Party C to conduct follow-up and\n407: confirmatory due diligence.\n408: On September 3, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and\n409: representatives of Party A participated in a telephone conference call for Party A to conduct follow-up and\n410: confirmatory due diligence.\n411: On September 9, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price\n412: of $19.50 per share, indicating that Pamplona was committed to provide 100% of the capital required to\n413: consummate the transaction and requesting that CSC/Pamplona and Mac-Gray agree to exclusive negotiations for\n414: two weeks. On September 9, 2013, Party B also submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash\n415: purchase price of $18.50 per share and proposed to finance the transaction with a combination of equity from\n416: affiliated funds and third party debt. Party B's revised indication of interest did not include a firm\n417: financing commitment.\n418: On September 10, 2013, Party A submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of\n419: $18.00 to $19.00 per share, to be financed by outstanding credit facilities or affiliated equity sources. Also\n420: on September 10, 2013, in a telephone call to a representative of BofA Merrill Lynch, a representative of\n421: Party C indicated that it was submitting a revised oral indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price\n422: of $16.00 to $17.00 per share. Neither Party A's nor Party C's revised indication of interest included a firm\n423: financing commitment.\n424: On September 11, 2013, members of the Transaction Committee, in a telephone call, without identifying Party A,\n425: Party B, Party C or CSC/Pamplona and without indicating any proposed purchase prices, provided Mr. MacDonald\n426: with a limited overview of the status of the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives, including\n427: the decision to engage in strategic alternatives process, and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and\n428: CSC/Pamplona so that Mr. MacDonald could consider whether he continued to have an interest in potentially\n429: rolling over any of his Mac-Gray shares in a transaction. During this call, Mr. MacDonald indicated that he\n430: had decided that he was not interested in rolling over any of his shares in any transaction.\n431: Later on September 11, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting to review the revised indications\n432: of interest submitted by Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. By invitation of the Special Committee,\n433: Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma, as well as representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter, were present\n434: for all or portions of the meeting. BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed with the Special Committee the current status\n435: of the revised indications of interest. The Special Committee also reviewed and discussed the Projections,\n436: including the risks and opportunities relating to Mac-Gray's ability to execute its strategic plan as an\n437: independent, stand-alone company. The Special Committee also considered the Projections in light of the\n438: revised indications of interest. BofA Merrill Lynch then indicated that, based on conversations with Party A,\n439: Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona, it was unlikely that any of these parties would move forward in the process\n440: without the express support of Mr. MacDonald. The Transaction Committee reported that Mr. MacDonald had\n441: 35\n442: \n443: ========== PAGE 36 (segment 41, zeq=[35]) ==========\n444: Table of Contents\n445: decided that he was not interested in rolling over any of his shares in a transaction and recommended that Mr.\n446: MacDonald be fully briefed of the process to date. The Special Committee agreed, noting that Mr. MacDonald's\n447: perspective on the purchase price of a sale transaction was critical to the Special Committee's evaluation, as\n448: any potential acquiror would be unlikely to proceed without knowing that Mr. MacDonald would support the\n449: transaction. Based on the amount of due diligence conducted by Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona, and\n450: discussions with such parties to date, and in an effort to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders, the\n451: Special Committee instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to call each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona to\n452: request final indications of interest by September 18, 2013 and to indicate that Mac-Gray would consider\n453: engaging in a short period of exclusive negotiations with one of the bidders depending upon, among other\n454: things, the proposed purchase price and the level of deal certainty indicated in the bidder's final proposal.\n455: As authorized and instructed by the Special Committee, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch called each of\n456: Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona on September 11, 2013 with such instructions.\n457: On September 12, 2013, at the instruction of the Transaction Committee, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch\n458: participated in a telephone conference call with Mr. MacDonald and provided him with a detailed overview of\n459: the strategic alternatives process, including the status of negotiations with and the identity of Party A,\n460: Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. Although Mr. MacDonald voiced his general support for continuing with the\n461: process, he expressed his view that Mac-Gray should continue to seek a higher value for stockholders.\n462: Between September 11, 2013 and September 18, 2013, Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona continued to\n463: conduct business and legal due diligence.\n464: On September 18, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a proposal for an all-cash purchase price of $20.75 per share,\n465: reiterating that Pamplona was committed to provide 100% of the capital required to consummate the transaction\n466: and requesting that CSC/Pamplona and Mac-Gray agree to engage in exclusive negotiations for two weeks. Also on\n467: September 18, 2013, Party A reiterated in a telephone call to a representative of BofA Merrill Lynch that its\n468: previous indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $18.00 to $19.00 per share was its best and\n469: final offer. On September 18, 2013, Party B also submitted a revised indication of interest with a purchase\n470: price that Party B valued at $21.50 per share, including $19.00 of cash to be paid at closing and the\n471: remaining per share price to be paid in the form of options to be issued to Mac-Gray stockholders,\n472: representing 10% of the new equity with a strike price equal to Party B's initial cost basis. These options,\n473: which we refer to as the Party B options, would vest on the achievement of the base case management\n474: projections (i.e., over five years), excluding any future acquisitions. Party B's proposal stated that it\n475: valued the Party B options at $2.50 of incremental value per share. Party C did not submit a revised\n476: indication of interest or reiterate its prior indication of interest of an all-cash purchase price of $16.00\n477: to $17.00 per share nor did it specify any reasons in connection therewith.\n478: On September 19, 2013, the Special Committee held an in-person meeting. By invitation of the Special\n479: Committee, Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Shea, as well as representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter,\n480: were also present for all or portions of the meeting. At this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch updated the Special\n481: Committee as to the current status of the revised indications of interest submitted by Party A, Party B and\n482: CSC/Pamplona and reported that Party C did not submit a revised indication of interest or reiterate its prior\n483: indication of interest. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported to the Special Committee on Mr. MacDonald's general\n484: support for continuing with the process and his view that Mac-Gray should continue to seek a higher value for\n485: stockholders. Mr. Shea again reviewed the Projections with the Special Committee and BofA Merrill Lynch. The\n486: Special Committee again discussed the Projections, including the risks relating to Mac-Gray's ability to\n487: execute its strategic plan as an independent, stand-alone company (including Mac-Gray's ability to identify,\n488: acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses, establish new and/or renew\n489: existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically dominated by smaller\n490: 36\n491: \n492: ========== PAGE 37 (segment 42, zeq=[36]) ==========\n493: Table of Contents\n494: local and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the\n495: general risks of market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price). The Special Committee also again\n496: discussed the potential opportunities if Mac-Gray were able to execute its strategic plan as an independent,\n497: stand-alone company (including the ability to improve profitability and EBITDA margins by increasing scale and\n498: density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as the ability to increase revenue\n499: through vend increases and the conversion from coin-operated technology to card-based technology). The Special\n500: Committee also discussed the risks involved if Mac-Gray did not execute on its strategic business plan,\n501: including the difficulties with increasing the Company's stock price without a significant or transformative\n502: transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring. The Special Committee then considered the\n503: Projections in light of the revised indications of interest. Representatives from Goodwin Procter again\n504: reviewed the Special Committee's fiduciary duties in the context of a sale of the company. The Special\n505: Committee then discussed each of the three revised proposals, including their advantages and disadvantages.\n506: The Special Committee discussed the risks and complexities of the Party B options, including their contingent\n507: and deferred nature and the uncertainties associated with how Mac-Gray's performance would be measured,\n508: especially in light of the fact that current Mac-Gray management would not be in control of the surviving\n509: corporation to monitor or drive the growth necessary to trigger payment under the Party B options.\n510: The representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch were then excused from the meeting and the Special Committee\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 511]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt, lines 251-510\n251: 50 parties, including CSC and Pamplona, who together we refer to as CSC/Pamplona, and Party A, to solicit\n252: potential interest in a transaction with Mac-Gray, 15 of which were potential strategic buyers and 35 of which\n253: were potential financial buyers, including private equity firms.\n254: During the next several weeks, in accordance with the directives of the Special Committee, BofA Merrill Lynch\n255: contacted the strategic and financial parties identified at the June 24th Special Committee meeting. Over the\n256: next two months a total of 20 potential bidders, including two strategic bidders (Party A and CSC/Pamplona)\n257: and 18 financial bidders (including Party B and Party C), entered into confidentiality agreements with Mac-\n258: Gray on a form provided by Mac-Gray, the terms of which were individually negotiated with potential bidders,\n259: but did not vary materially from the form provided. The confidentiality agreements contained customary non-\n260: disclosure provisions and a customary standstill provision that either expired or permitted the potential\n261: bidder to make confidential proposals to Mac-Gray (a so-called \"sunset\" provision) upon announcement of a\n262: business combination between Mac-Gray and a third party, and, in some instances upon announcement of a change\n263: in control. Parties that had entered into confidentiality and standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were\n264: furnished an informational package for the purpose of providing potential bidders with data to formulate a\n265: preliminary indication of interest, which they were instructed to submit by July 23, 2013. Parties that had\n266: entered into confidentiality and standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were furnished an informational package\n267: for the purpose of providing potential bidders with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest,\n268: which they were instructed to submit by July 23, 2013. The letter requested that the proposals address, among\n269: other things, valuation, sources and structures of financing, material conditions, including required\n270: approvals, and focus areas for due diligence.\n271: On June 28, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee participated in a telephone conference call with\n272: representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter. BofA Merrill Lynch summarized the contacts they had\n273: had with certain of the potential bidders. In particular, BofA Merrill Lynch reported that they had spoken\n274: with representatives of Party A, as instructed by the Special Committee, who had expressed the view that there\n275: would be considerable synergies if Party A were to acquire Mac-Gray and that Party A would be speaking with\n276: certain financing sources with respect to funding such a transaction. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported that\n277: they had spoken with representatives of Pamplona, as instructed by the Special Committee, who expressed\n278: interest in a transaction, including an acquisition of Mac-Gray by its portfolio company, CSC. Pamplona\n279: emphasized that business and legal due diligence would be minimal given its familiarity with Mac-Gray and the\n280: industry generally.\n281: Later on June 28, 2013, Party B, entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was\n282: provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party B with data to formulate a\n283: preliminary indication of interest.\n284: On June 30, 2013, Party C entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was\n285: provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party C with data to formulate a\n286: preliminary indication of interest.\n287: On July 3, 2013, Mr. Rothenberg called a representative of Goodwin Procter to indicate that he had spoken with\n288: representatives of Pamplona about Moab's desire to maintain the flexibility to consider a possible equity\n289: rollover if Pamplona were to participate in a business combination with Mac-Gray. Mr. Rothenberg also informed\n290: Goodwin Procter that Pamplona would be requesting that any confidentiality agreement CSC/Pamplona execute with\n291: Mac-Gray permit CSC and Pamplona to discuss their bid and evaluation material with Moab, with Moab agreeing to\n292: exclusively engage with CSC/Pamplona.\n293: On July 6, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting for the purpose of receiving an update on the\n294: ongoing sale process. By invitation of the Special Committee, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and\n295: Goodwin Procter were also present throughout the meeting. The Special Committee discussed whether Moab should\n296: be permitted to partner exclusively with CSC/Pamplona. Because the\n297: 32\n298: \n299: ========== PAGE 33 (segment 38, zeq=[32]) ==========\n300: Table of Contents\n301: objective of the sale process was to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders, the Special Committee decided\n302: to deny the exclusivity request at this stage of the process so that the potential equity rollover of Moab's\n303: shares could be available as a source of capital for other potential bidders. The Special Committee instructed\n304: BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter to communicate to CSC/Pamplona and Mr. Rothenberg, respectively, that\n305: it was premature for Mac-Gray to permit Moab to engage in equity rollover discussions with any party,\n306: including CSC/Pamplona, but that such discussions would be permitted at a later stage of the sale process.\n307: On July 11, 2013, CSC/Pamplona entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and were\n308: provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing CSC/Pamplona with data to formulate a\n309: preliminary indication of interest.\n310: Between July 11, 2013 and July 23, 2013, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona engaged in a preliminary review of\n311: Mac-Gray and the potential acquisition opportunity. Also during this time, outside counsel to Party A and\n312: representatives of Goodwin Procter negotiated the terms of a confidentiality and standstill agreement, but did\n313: not come to agreement on the terms of the customer and employee non-solicitation provisions.\n314: On July 23, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of\n315: $18.50 per share.\n316: On July 24, 2013, Party B submitted a preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of\n317: $17.00 to $18.00 per share. Also on July 24, 2013, representatives from Party C called BofA Merrill Lynch and\n318: presented an oral preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $15.00 to $17.00 per\n319: share.\n320: On July 25, 2013, the Board held a regularly scheduled in-person meeting. By invitation of the Board, Mr. Shea\n321: and representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also present for all or portions of this\n322: meeting. Prior to the start of the meeting, because the Board expected to discuss matters related to the sale\n323: process, and in light of Moab's continuing interest in a potential equity rollover in any sale transaction,\n324: Mr. Rothenberg recused himself from the meeting and the meeting proceeded as a meeting of the Special\n325: Committee. At this meeting, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed the preliminary indications of\n326: interest received from Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona as well as the Party A June 21 proposal, in each case\n327: from a financial point of view. The Board reviewed management's five-year strategic business plan and\n328: Projections. During this meeting, representatives of Goodwin Procter reviewed for the Special Committee its\n329: fiduciary duties in the context of considering a potential sale. The Special Committee then discussed the\n330: potential conflict of interest arising from Moab's continuing desire to consider an equity rollover and\n331: decided that Mr. Rothenberg should be asked to recuse himself from participating in Board meetings for the\n332: duration of the sale process so he would not be privy to confidential information about Mac-Gray and our\n333: business.\n334: The Special Committee discussed that the preliminary indications of interest were lower than expected and\n335: suggested that additional due diligence, including with regard to the potential synergies, should result in\n336: improved offers by the strategic parties. The Special Committee then discussed the highly competitive nature\n337: of Mac-Gray's industry and expressed concern over permitting access to and the potential misuse of sensitive\n338: and competitive information, including customer terms and locations, especially by strategic bidders.\n339: Accordingly, the Special Committee concluded that it would be advisable to stage the disclosure of such\n340: information to each of the bidders to the extent that a bidder's indication of interest and other actions\n341: demonstrated its seriousness in acquiring Mac-Gray on terms viewed favorably by the Special Committee. Based\n342: on this discussion, BofA Merrill Lynch discussed with the Special Committee a proposed process for the second\n343: stage of the on-going sale process. As part of this review, BofA Merrill Lynch recommended that management\n344: meetings be arranged between members of Mac-Gray management (other than Mr. MacDonald) and each of Party A (if\n345: Party A\n346: 33\n347: \n348: ========== PAGE 34 (segment 39, zeq=[33]) ==========\n349: Table of Contents\n350: agreed to enter into a confidentiality agreement), Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona to provide these parties\n351: with access to high level information concerning Mac-Gray's technology, pipeline, management team, regional\n352: operations, contracts, operations and growth initiatives, and in the case of CSC/Pamplona and Party A,\n353: synergies, and to then request that each of these parties submit a revised indication of interest. After\n354: discussion and in an effort to drive offers with increased value, the Special Committee authorized and\n355: instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to proceed with the staged disclosure as discussed at this meeting and to reach\n356: out to each of the four interested bidders and arrange management meetings.\n357: Later on July 25, 2013, Party C submitted a written indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of\n358: $16.00 to $16.50 per share.\n359: Discussions with Party A resumed on July 27, 2103 and Party A and the Transaction Committee agreed to the\n360: terms of the customer and employee non-solicitation provisions. On August 5, 2013, Party A entered into a\n361: confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the\n362: purpose of providing Party A with data to potentially improve the terms of the Party A June 21 proposal.\n363: On August 6, 2013, representatives of Party B attended a management presentation at the Boston office of\n364: Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and a member of the\n365: Transaction Committee.\n366: On August 8, 2013, representatives of Party C attended a management presentation at the Boston office of\n367: Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and a member of the\n368: Transaction Committee.\n369: On August 12, 2013, representatives of CSC and Pamplona attended a management presentation at the Boston\n370: office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and the\n371: two members of the Transaction Committee.\n372: On August 14, 2013, representatives of Party A attended a management presentation at the Boston office of\n373: Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and the two members\n374: of the Transaction Committee.\n375: On August 15, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting for the purpose of reviewing the on-going\n376: strategic alternatives process. By invitation of the Special Committee, representatives from BofA Merrill\n377: Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also in attendance. BofA Merrill Lynch updated the Special Committee as to the\n378: current status of the strategic alternatives process, including the due diligence process and the management\n379: presentations with Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. After discussion and as discussed at the July\n380: 25th Special Committee meeting, the Special Committee authorized and instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to send a\n381: letter to each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona once all four of the bidders' due diligence was\n382: substantially complete requesting that, in light of the results of the respective party's due diligence, such\n383: party submit a revised written proposal in order to continue in the process, including to engage in more in-\n384: depth and confirmatory due diligence of Mac-Gray.\n385: Between August 6, 2013 and August 27, 2013, the four bidders conducted business and legal due diligence.\n386: During this time, Party B and Party C, in light of the fact that they were financial bidders, were given broad\n387: access to an electronic data site, and Party A and CSC/Pamplona, in light of the fact that they were strategic\n388: bidders, were given somewhat limited access to this electronic data site, with access to customer data,\n389: including customer locations, being reserved until later in the process as part of the staging discussed at\n390: the July 25th Special Committee meeting. During this time, both Party A and CSC/Pamplona participated in\n391: discussions with members of Mac-Gray management around synergies.\n392: 34\n393: \n394: ========== PAGE 35 (segment 40, zeq=[34]) ==========\n395: Table of Contents\n396: On August 27, 2013, BofA Merrill Lynch, as instructed by the Special Committee, sent a letter to each of Party\n397: A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona instructing such bidders to submit revised written proposals no later\n398: than September 9, 2013 if such bidders wished to proceed further in the process to pursue an in-depth\n399: evaluation of Mac-Gray with the objective of making a revised offer. The letter requested that the revised\n400: proposals address, among other things, valuation, sources and structures of financing, regulatory approvals\n401: and other post-signing covenants and focus areas for due diligence.\n402: Also on August 27, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and\n403: representatives of Party B participated in a telephone conference call for Party B to conduct follow-up and\n404: confirmatory due diligence.\n405: On August 29, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and\n406: representatives of Party C participated in an in-person session for Party C to conduct follow-up and\n407: confirmatory due diligence.\n408: On September 3, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and\n409: representatives of Party A participated in a telephone conference call for Party A to conduct follow-up and\n410: confirmatory due diligence.\n411: On September 9, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price\n412: of $19.50 per share, indicating that Pamplona was committed to provide 100% of the capital required to\n413: consummate the transaction and requesting that CSC/Pamplona and Mac-Gray agree to exclusive negotiations for\n414: two weeks. On September 9, 2013, Party B also submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash\n415: purchase price of $18.50 per share and proposed to finance the transaction with a combination of equity from\n416: affiliated funds and third party debt. Party B's revised indication of interest did not include a firm\n417: financing commitment.\n418: On September 10, 2013, Party A submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of\n419: $18.00 to $19.00 per share, to be financed by outstanding credit facilities or affiliated equity sources. Also\n420: on September 10, 2013, in a telephone call to a representative of BofA Merrill Lynch, a representative of\n421: Party C indicated that it was submitting a revised oral indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price\n422: of $16.00 to $17.00 per share. Neither Party A's nor Party C's revised indication of interest included a firm\n423: financing commitment.\n424: On September 11, 2013, members of the Transaction Committee, in a telephone call, without identifying Party A,\n425: Party B, Party C or CSC/Pamplona and without indicating any proposed purchase prices, provided Mr. MacDonald\n426: with a limited overview of the status of the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives, including\n427: the decision to engage in strategic alternatives process, and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and\n428: CSC/Pamplona so that Mr. MacDonald could consider whether he continued to have an interest in potentially\n429: rolling over any of his Mac-Gray shares in a transaction. During this call, Mr. MacDonald indicated that he\n430: had decided that he was not interested in rolling over any of his shares in any transaction.\n431: Later on September 11, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting to review the revised indications\n432: of interest submitted by Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. By invitation of the Special Committee,\n433: Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma, as well as representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter, were present\n434: for all or portions of the meeting. BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed with the Special Committee the current status\n435: of the revised indications of interest. The Special Committee also reviewed and discussed the Projections,\n436: including the risks and opportunities relating to Mac-Gray's ability to execute its strategic plan as an\n437: independent, stand-alone company. The Special Committee also considered the Projections in light of the\n438: revised indications of interest. BofA Merrill Lynch then indicated that, based on conversations with Party A,\n439: Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona, it was unlikely that any of these parties would move forward in the process\n440: without the express support of Mr. MacDonald. The Transaction Committee reported that Mr. MacDonald had\n441: 35\n442: \n443: ========== PAGE 36 (segment 41, zeq=[35]) ==========\n444: Table of Contents\n445: decided that he was not interested in rolling over any of his shares in a transaction and recommended that Mr.\n446: MacDonald be fully briefed of the process to date. The Special Committee agreed, noting that Mr. MacDonald's\n447: perspective on the purchase price of a sale transaction was critical to the Special Committee's evaluation, as\n448: any potential acquiror would be unlikely to proceed without knowing that Mr. MacDonald would support the\n449: transaction. Based on the amount of due diligence conducted by Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona, and\n450: discussions with such parties to date, and in an effort to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders, the\n451: Special Committee instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to call each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona to\n452: request final indications of interest by September 18, 2013 and to indicate that Mac-Gray would consider\n453: engaging in a short period of exclusive negotiations with one of the bidders depending upon, among other\n454: things, the proposed purchase price and the level of deal certainty indicated in the bidder's final proposal.\n455: As authorized and instructed by the Special Committee, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch called each of\n456: Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona on September 11, 2013 with such instructions.\n457: On September 12, 2013, at the instruction of the Transaction Committee, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch\n458: participated in a telephone conference call with Mr. MacDonald and provided him with a detailed overview of\n459: the strategic alternatives process, including the status of negotiations with and the identity of Party A,\n460: Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. Although Mr. MacDonald voiced his general support for continuing with the\n461: process, he expressed his view that Mac-Gray should continue to seek a higher value for stockholders.\n462: Between September 11, 2013 and September 18, 2013, Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona continued to\n463: conduct business and legal due diligence.\n464: On September 18, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a proposal for an all-cash purchase price of $20.75 per share,\n465: reiterating that Pamplona was committed to provide 100% of the capital required to consummate the transaction\n466: and requesting that CSC/Pamplona and Mac-Gray agree to engage in exclusive negotiations for two weeks. Also on\n467: September 18, 2013, Party A reiterated in a telephone call to a representative of BofA Merrill Lynch that its\n468: previous indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $18.00 to $19.00 per share was its best and\n469: final offer. On September 18, 2013, Party B also submitted a revised indication of interest with a purchase\n470: price that Party B valued at $21.50 per share, including $19.00 of cash to be paid at closing and the\n471: remaining per share price to be paid in the form of options to be issued to Mac-Gray stockholders,\n472: representing 10% of the new equity with a strike price equal to Party B's initial cost basis. These options,\n473: which we refer to as the Party B options, would vest on the achievement of the base case management\n474: projections (i.e., over five years), excluding any future acquisitions. Party B's proposal stated that it\n475: valued the Party B options at $2.50 of incremental value per share. Party C did not submit a revised\n476: indication of interest or reiterate its prior indication of interest of an all-cash purchase price of $16.00\n477: to $17.00 per share nor did it specify any reasons in connection therewith.\n478: On September 19, 2013, the Special Committee held an in-person meeting. By invitation of the Special\n479: Committee, Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Shea, as well as representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter,\n480: were also present for all or portions of the meeting. At this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch updated the Special\n481: Committee as to the current status of the revised indications of interest submitted by Party A, Party B and\n482: CSC/Pamplona and reported that Party C did not submit a revised indication of interest or reiterate its prior\n483: indication of interest. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported to the Special Committee on Mr. MacDonald's general\n484: support for continuing with the process and his view that Mac-Gray should continue to seek a higher value for\n485: stockholders. Mr. Shea again reviewed the Projections with the Special Committee and BofA Merrill Lynch. The\n486: Special Committee again discussed the Projections, including the risks relating to Mac-Gray's ability to\n487: execute its strategic plan as an independent, stand-alone company (including Mac-Gray's ability to identify,\n488: acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses, establish new and/or renew\n489: existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically dominated by smaller\n490: 36\n491: \n492: ========== PAGE 37 (segment 42, zeq=[36]) ==========\n493: Table of Contents\n494: local and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the\n495: general risks of market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price). The Special Committee also again\n496: discussed the potential opportunities if Mac-Gray were able to execute its strategic plan as an independent,\n497: stand-alone company (including the ability to improve profitability and EBITDA margins by increasing scale and\n498: density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as the ability to increase revenue\n499: through vend increases and the conversion from coin-operated technology to card-based technology). The Special\n500: Committee also discussed the risks involved if Mac-Gray did not execute on its strategic business plan,\n501: including the difficulties with increasing the Company's stock price without a significant or transformative\n502: transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring. The Special Committee then considered the\n503: Projections in light of the revised indications of interest. Representatives from Goodwin Procter again\n504: reviewed the Special Committee's fiduciary duties in the context of a sale of the company. The Special\n505: Committee then discussed each of the three revised proposals, including their advantages and disadvantages.\n506: The Special Committee discussed the risks and complexities of the Party B options, including their contingent\n507: and deferred nature and the uncertainties associated with how Mac-Gray's performance would be measured,\n508: especially in light of the fact that current Mac-Gray management would not be in control of the surviving\n509: corporation to monitor or drive the growth necessary to trigger payment under the Party B options.\n510: The representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch were then excused from the meeting and the Special Committee\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 511]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 260, \"offset\": 251, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824341438,"end":1789824341608}}}}
