{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824343664,"sessionID":"ses_f462a5515ffeVXejhZ4OL349Hs","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d81dd3001yWEy3UA58P5hlF","sessionID":"ses_f462a5515ffeVXejhZ4OL349Hs","messageID":"msg_0b9d81672001X3clAu7jMpsyaO","type":"tool","id":"call_00_ET_65MEi7toFhpNwjFAppjw4272","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":220,"offset":511,"path":"/tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt, lines 511-726\n511: continued its discussion. The Special Committee noted that the indication of interest submitted by Party B,\n512: including the Party B options, was on its face higher than the CSC/Pamplona proposal but further discussed the\n513: risks and complexities involved with measuring and achieving the performance required to vest the Party B\n514: options. The Special Committee also noted that Party B would likely be financing the transaction with a\n515: combination of equity from affiliated funds and third party debt capital, which would involve more risk than\n516: the Pamplona-funded CSC/Pamplona proposal, which did not require any third party financing. Following a\n517: discussion, the Special Committee concluded that the revised indication of interest submitted by CSC/Pamplona\n518: had the potential of delivering the most value to stockholders, provided greater certainty and involved less\n519: potential risk than the other indications. However, the Special Committee also concluded that, primarily in\n520: view of potential synergies between Mac-Gray and CSC, CSC/Pamplona should be in a position to increase their\n521: offer price above $20.75. The Special Committee then authorized the Transaction Committee to inform\n522: CSC/Pamplona that the Special Committee would be prepared to move into exclusivity at a price of $21.25 per\n523: share and authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter to negotiate the terms of such exclusivity\n524: based on guidance provided by the Special Committee. In addition, at this meeting Mr. MacDonald indicated that\n525: he would be supportive of a price of $21.25 per share.\n526: On September 21, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee called the representative of CSC/Pamplona to\n527: propose that Mac-Gray was prepared to move forward with CSC/Pamplona on an exclusive basis for two weeks\n528: subject to CSC/Pamplona increasing their all-cash purchase price to $21.25 per share. Later that same day, the\n529: representative of CSC/Pamplona called the members of the Transaction Committee to confirm that CSC/Pamplona\n530: were willing to increase their all-cash purchase price to $21.25 per share as a last and best offer, provided\n531: that Mac-Gray agree to a two week period of exclusive negotiations. The representative of CSC/Pamplona also\n532: requested that shares of Mac-Gray common stock owned beneficially and of record by Mr. MacDonald and his\n533: immediate family be subject to a customary voting agreement, which we refer to as the MacDonald voting\n534: agreements.\n535: Between September 21, 2013 and September 23, 2013, the parties negotiated the terms of the exclusivity\n536: agreement, including an expiration date of October 12, 2013, the inclusion of a $15 million \"reverse\"\n537: termination fee payable by CSC/Pamplona to Mac-Gray in the event regulatory clearance is not obtained, which\n538: we refer to as the CSC reverse termination fee, agreements regarding certain post-signing covenants, the fact\n539: that there would be no financing contingency and that Pamplona was\n540: 37\n541: \n542: ========== PAGE 38 (segment 43, zeq=[37]) ==========\n543: Table of Contents\n544: committing to fund 100% of the capital needed for closing, which, together with the all-cash per share\n545: purchase price of $21.25, we refer to as the CSC/Pamplona final proposal. The parties also agreed that the\n546: exclusivity agreement would automatically terminate if CSC or Pamplona adversely changed any of the terms of\n547: the CSC/Pamplona final proposal.\n548: On September 23, 2013, representatives from Goodwin Procter participated in a telephone conference call with\n549: Mr. Rothenberg and his counsel to inform Mr. Rothenberg, on a limited basis, of the status of the Special\n550: Committee's review of strategic alternatives and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and CSC and Pamplona to\n551: provide Moab with the opportunity to resume discussions with CSC/Pamplona regarding a possible equity rollover\n552: of Moab's shares in the transaction if Moab still had an interest in such a rollover. Mr. Rothenberg indicated\n553: he would like to discuss the possibility of an equity rollover with CSC/Pamplona and it was agreed that\n554: representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch would arrange and facilitate such a discussion.\n555: Later on September 23, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to\n556: receive an update on the negotiations from the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter regarding the\n557: CSC/Pamplona final proposal and request for exclusivity. Representatives from Goodwin Procter reviewed the\n558: terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal with the Special Committee, including the $21.25 per share cash\n559: consideration, the $15 million CSC reverse termination fee, certain post-signing covenants, the fact that\n560: there would be no financing contingency and that Pamplona was committing to fund 100% of the capital needed\n561: for closing. After discussion, the Special Committee authorized Mac-Gray to enter into an exclusivity\n562: agreement providing for exclusive negotiations until 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013 on the terms included in\n563: the CSC/Pamplona final proposal and authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter to continue\n564: negotiations with CSC/Pamplona based on guidance provided by the Special Committee at this meeting.\n565: On September 24, 2013, CSC, Pamplona and Mac-Gray executed the exclusivity agreement providing for exclusive\n566: negotiations until 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013, subject to certain conditions, including that CSC and\n567: Pamplona not adversely change or modify the terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal.\n568: On September 25, 2013, Goodwin Procter delivered to Kirkland & Ellis LLP, outside legal counsel to\n569: CSC/Pamplona, whom we refer to as Kirkland, the initial draft of the merger agreement. Between September 25,\n570: 2013 and October 7, 2013, the Transaction Committee and representatives from CSC/Pamplona, Goodwin Procter and\n571: Kirkland negotiated the terms of the merger agreement (including our ability to respond to unsolicited\n572: inquiries following the announcement of the transaction and our right to terminate the merger agreement to\n573: accept a superior proposal) and the Pamplona commitment letter. The parties discussed various open points in\n574: the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including the proposal by Mac-Gray that both Pamplona\n575: and CSC be responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, including liability for any and all\n576: damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain post-signing covenants and the parties'\n577: termination rights, including the amount of the termination fee payable by Mac-Gray to CSC under certain\n578: circumstances (which we refer to as the Mac-Gray termination fee), which Mac-Gray proposed should approximate\n579: 2.5% of the total equity value of the transaction. Also during this time, CSC and Pamplona were granted full\n580: access to the electronic data site and access to certain members of management and conducted confirmatory\n581: business and legal due diligence on Mac-Gray. Mr. Rothenberg engaged in various discussions with\n582: representatives of Pamplona during this time to discuss a possible rollover of Moab shares. As a result of\n583: such discussions, it was determined that Moab would not rollover any Moab shares in the transaction. Following\n584: such determination, BofA Merrill Lynch and the Transaction Committee provided Mr. Rothenberg with the details\n585: regarding the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and\n586: CSC/Pamplona.\n587: 38\n588: \n589: ========== PAGE 39 (segment 44, zeq=[38]) ==========\n590: Table of Contents\n591: Between September 24, 2013 and September 27, 2013, Kirkland and outside counsel to Mr. MacDonald negotiated\n592: the terms of the MacDonald voting agreements, which were entered into by Mr. MacDonald, his wife and one of\n593: his trusts on September 27, 2013, and which became effective upon entry into the merger agreement.\n594: On October 1, 2013, representatives of Mac-Gray management, along with representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch\n595: and representatives from CSC/Pamplona attended a meeting at the Boston offices of Goodwin Procter for purposes\n596: of conducting confirmatory business due diligence. Representatives of Mac-Gray, Goodwin Procter, BofA Merrill\n597: Lynch and representatives of CSC/Pamplona and Kirkland participated in various telephone conference calls over\n598: the subsequent days to finalize business and legal due diligence.\n599: On October 5, 2013, Kirkland delivered to Goodwin Procter a revised draft of the Pamplona commitment letter\n600: which, among other things, proposed that, in addition to CSC, Pamplona would be responsible in the event CSC\n601: failed to consummate the merger, but that Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the\n602: merger agreement would be limited to an unspecified dollar amount.\n603: On October 7, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive an\n604: update on the transaction negotiations from the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter. By invitation of\n605: the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald was also present for a portion of the meeting. Mr. Daoust reported to the\n606: Special Committee that, after discussions between Moab and representatives of Pamplona, it was determined that\n607: Moab would not rollover any Moab shares in the transaction. Mr. Daoust also provided the Special Committee\n608: with an overview of the open business points and the progress the parties had made since the September 23rd\n609: Special Committee meeting. Representatives from Goodwin Procter then reported on the progress that had been\n610: made in diligence and summarized the open issues in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter,\n611: including to what extent Pamplona would be responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger,\n612: including CSC's and Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain\n613: post-signing covenants and the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination\n614: fee and the CSC reverse termination fee. The Special Committee authorized the Transaction Committee and\n615: Goodwin Procter to continue negotiations with CSC/Pamplona, and, among other things, provided guidance with\n616: respect to the open transaction issues discussed at the meeting.\n617: Also on October 7, 2013, Kirkland delivered to Goodwin Procter a revised draft of the merger agreement which,\n618: among other things, proposed a $15 million Mac-Gray termination fee, which represented approximately 4.4% of\n619: the total equity value of the transaction, and certain positions on the scope of various post-signing\n620: covenants.\n621: On October 8, 2013, representatives from Goodwin Procter and Kirkland participated in a telephone conference\n622: call to discuss the open points in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including to what\n623: extent Pamplona would also have liability in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, which Kirkland\n624: proposed, at the direction of CSC/Pamplona, be capped at $50 million. Kirkland and Goodwin Procter also\n625: discussed CSC's and Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain\n626: post-signing covenants and the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination\n627: fee and the CSC reverse termination fee. The parties also discussed potential compromises on the post-signing\n628: covenants. Goodwin Procter and Kirkland agreed to communicate these positions to their respective clients.\n629: Later that day, after various discussions among representatives from CSC/Pamplona, Kirkland, Goodwin Procter\n630: and the members of the Transaction Committee, and on behalf of the Special Committee, Goodwin Procter sent an\n631: e-mail to the representatives of Kirkland that proposed to accept the\n632: 39\n633: \n634: ========== PAGE 40 (segment 45, zeq=[39]) ==========\n635: Table of Contents\n636: $50 million liability cap proposed by Pamplona and proposed a $10.5 million Mac-Gray termination fee, which\n637: represented approximately 3.0% of the total equity value of the transaction.\n638: On October 9, 2013, in follow up to the October 8, 2013 negotiations and proposals made by Goodwin Procter,\n639: the members of the Transaction Committee participated in a telephone conference call with a representative\n640: from CSC/Pamplona to discuss the open issues in the merger agreement, including certain post-signing covenants\n641: and the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the CSC reverse\n642: termination fee, but did not reach agreement on these issues.\n643: Later on October 9, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive\n644: an update on the transaction negotiations from the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter. By invitation of\n645: the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald was also present at this meeting. The Transaction Committee reported to\n646: the Special Committee on the negotiations that had taken place since the meeting of the Special Committee held\n647: on October 7, 2013. The Transaction Committee reported that the parties had come to an agreement that\n648: Pamplona's liability for certain damages would be limited to $50 million, but that certain post-signing\n649: covenants and the amounts of the termination fees remained open issues. The Special Committee discussed\n650: various alternatives that could be proposed to CSC/Pamplona on the open issues. Goodwin Procter then reported\n651: that Kirkland had requested on behalf of CSC/Pamplona that the exclusivity agreement, due to expire at 5 p.m.\n652: ET on October 12, 2013, be extended to allow for continued negotiations of these open issues. After\n653: discussion, the Special Committee authorized the Transaction Committee to negotiate the open issues within\n654: certain parameters and in connection therewith to extend exclusivity to October 15, 2013. The Special\n655: Committee also determined to reconvene for an update telephone conference call on October 11, 2013.\n656: On October 10, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee and the representative of CSC/Pamplona engaged\n657: in various discussions regarding the open issues, including certain post-signing covenants and the amounts of\n658: the termination fees. While the parties reached a compromise on the post-signing covenants, no agreement was\n659: reached on the amount of the Mac-Gray termination fee. On October 11, 2013, the Special Committee held a\n660: telephonic meeting to discuss the proposed terms of the transaction and the status of discussions with\n661: CSC/Pamplona. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Rothenberg and representatives of Goodwin Procter\n662: were also present. The members of the Transaction Committee reviewed with the Special Committee the\n663: negotiations that had taken place with representatives from CSC/Pamplona since the October 9th Special\n664: Committee meeting. The Transaction Committee reported that CSC/Pamplona, on the one hand, and the Transaction\n665: Committee (as authorized by the Special Committee at the October 9th meeting), on the other hand, had come to\n666: an agreement on certain post-signing covenants but that the parties had not reached agreement on the amount of\n667: the Mac-Gray termination fee. The Special Committee also discussed the fact that the exclusivity agreement\n668: would be expiring at 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013 and that CSC/Pamplona had requested an extension of\n669: exclusivity until the end of the day on October 15, 2013. After discussions, the Special Committee determined\n670: that the members of the Transaction Committee and representatives of Goodwin Procter should communicate to the\n671: representative of CSC/Pamplona and representatives at Kirkland, respectively, that the Special Committee\n672: continued to object to the $15 million Mac-Gray termination fee proposed by CSC/Pamplona and would not be\n673: willing to extend exclusivity unless a lower amount were agreed to. The Special Committee confirmed that the\n674: Transaction Committee and representatives of Mac-Gray were authorized to extend the exclusivity agreement\n675: until 11:59 p.m. on ET on October 15, 2013 if CSC/Pamplona would agree to a Mac-Gray termination fee of not\n676: more than $11 million, which represented approximately 3.2% of the total equity value of the transaction.\n677: Later on October 11, 2013, the two members of the Transaction Committee telephoned CSC/Pamplona and\n678: communicated the Special Committee's position. Shortly following that conversation, the representative of\n679: CSC/Pamplona telephoned Mr. Daoust and agreed to the $11 million Mac-Gray\n680: 40\n681: \n682: ========== PAGE 41 (segment 46, zeq=[40]) ==========\n683: Table of Contents\n684: termination fee. Following such agreement, Goodwin Procter delivered Kirkland revised drafts of the merger\n685: agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter.\n686: On October 12, 2013, CSC, Pamplona and Mac-Gray executed a letter agreement extending exclusivity until 11:59\n687: p.m. ET on October 15, 2013.\n688: Between October 12, 2013 and October 14, 2013, Kirkland and Goodwin Procter finalized the remaining unresolved\n689: issues in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter.\n690: Between October 8, 2013 and October 14, 2013, Kirkland and counsel for Moab negotiated the terms of a voting\n691: agreement for Moab.\n692: On October 14, 2013, the Special Committee held a meeting via teleconference to discuss the final terms of the\n693: transaction and the proposed definitive merger agreement and related documents. By invitation of the Special\n694: Committee, Mr. Rothenberg and representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also present for\n695: all or portions of the meeting. The members of the Transaction Committee reported to the Special Committee on\n696: the negotiations that had taken place since the meeting of the Special Committee held on October 11, 2013, and\n697: in particular that the Transaction Committee and CSC had come to an agreement on the remaining open business\n698: points, including the post-signing covenants and the termination fees. Goodwin Procter again reviewed with the\n699: Special Committee its fiduciary duties and provided a summary of the terms of the proposed merger agreement\n700: and the Pamplona capital commitment letter, copies of which were provided to the directors prior to the\n701: meeting. Also at this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed with the Special Committee its financial analysis\n702: of the consideration and delivered to the Special Committee an oral opinion, which was confirmed by delivery\n703: of a written opinion dated, October 14, 2013, to the effect that, as of that date and based on and subject to\n704: the assumptions and limitations described in its opinion, the consideration to be received by holders of Mac-\n705: Gray common stock was fair, from a financial point of view, to such holders. Following these presentations,\n706: the Special Committee went into an executive session that excluded BofA Merrill Lynch and, after discussion,\n707: the Special Committee unanimously determined to recommend to the Board that the merger agreement and the\n708: merger were advisable and in the best interests of Mac-Gray's stockholders and that the Board approve the\n709: merger on the terms and subject to the conditions set forth in the merger agreement, adopt the merger\n710: agreement and recommend that the Mac-Gray stockholders adopt and approve the merger agreement. The Special\n711: Committee meeting then adjourned.\n712: Following the adjournment of the Special Committee meeting, a meeting of the Mac-Gray Board of Directors\n713: convened. All members of the Board participated. Representatives from Goodwin Procter led a discussion on the\n714: terms of the merger agreement and advised the Board regarding the likely timing for execution of a definitive\n715: agreement and public announcement of the transaction and a projected closing timetable should the Board\n716: approve the merger. After discussion, the Board (upon the recommendation of the Special Committee) unanimously\n717: determined that the merger agreement and the merger were advisable and in the best interests of Mac-Gray's\n718: stockholders, approved the merger on the terms and subject to the conditions set forth in the merger\n719: agreement, adopted the merger agreement and recommended that the Mac-Gray stockholders adopt and approve the\n720: merger agreement.\n721: Later in the day, on October 14, 2013, the merger agreement was executed, the Pamplona commitment letter was\n722: delivered, Moab and Parent executed the Moab voting agreement and the Mac-Donald voting agreements became\n723: effective.\n724: On October 15, 2013, before the U.S. stock markets opened, Mac-Gray and CSC jointly announced the execution of\n725: the merger agreement.\n726: 41","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/background_wrapped.txt, lines 511-726\n511: continued its discussion. The Special Committee noted that the indication of interest submitted by Party B,\n512: including the Party B options, was on its face higher than the CSC/Pamplona proposal but further discussed the\n513: risks and complexities involved with measuring and achieving the performance required to vest the Party B\n514: options. The Special Committee also noted that Party B would likely be financing the transaction with a\n515: combination of equity from affiliated funds and third party debt capital, which would involve more risk than\n516: the Pamplona-funded CSC/Pamplona proposal, which did not require any third party financing. Following a\n517: discussion, the Special Committee concluded that the revised indication of interest submitted by CSC/Pamplona\n518: had the potential of delivering the most value to stockholders, provided greater certainty and involved less\n519: potential risk than the other indications. However, the Special Committee also concluded that, primarily in\n520: view of potential synergies between Mac-Gray and CSC, CSC/Pamplona should be in a position to increase their\n521: offer price above $20.75. The Special Committee then authorized the Transaction Committee to inform\n522: CSC/Pamplona that the Special Committee would be prepared to move into exclusivity at a price of $21.25 per\n523: share and authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter to negotiate the terms of such exclusivity\n524: based on guidance provided by the Special Committee. In addition, at this meeting Mr. MacDonald indicated that\n525: he would be supportive of a price of $21.25 per share.\n526: On September 21, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee called the representative of CSC/Pamplona to\n527: propose that Mac-Gray was prepared to move forward with CSC/Pamplona on an exclusive basis for two weeks\n528: subject to CSC/Pamplona increasing their all-cash purchase price to $21.25 per share. Later that same day, the\n529: representative of CSC/Pamplona called the members of the Transaction Committee to confirm that CSC/Pamplona\n530: were willing to increase their all-cash purchase price to $21.25 per share as a last and best offer, provided\n531: that Mac-Gray agree to a two week period of exclusive negotiations. The representative of CSC/Pamplona also\n532: requested that shares of Mac-Gray common stock owned beneficially and of record by Mr. MacDonald and his\n533: immediate family be subject to a customary voting agreement, which we refer to as the MacDonald voting\n534: agreements.\n535: Between September 21, 2013 and September 23, 2013, the parties negotiated the terms of the exclusivity\n536: agreement, including an expiration date of October 12, 2013, the inclusion of a $15 million \"reverse\"\n537: termination fee payable by CSC/Pamplona to Mac-Gray in the event regulatory clearance is not obtained, which\n538: we refer to as the CSC reverse termination fee, agreements regarding certain post-signing covenants, the fact\n539: that there would be no financing contingency and that Pamplona was\n540: 37\n541: \n542: ========== PAGE 38 (segment 43, zeq=[37]) ==========\n543: Table of Contents\n544: committing to fund 100% of the capital needed for closing, which, together with the all-cash per share\n545: purchase price of $21.25, we refer to as the CSC/Pamplona final proposal. The parties also agreed that the\n546: exclusivity agreement would automatically terminate if CSC or Pamplona adversely changed any of the terms of\n547: the CSC/Pamplona final proposal.\n548: On September 23, 2013, representatives from Goodwin Procter participated in a telephone conference call with\n549: Mr. Rothenberg and his counsel to inform Mr. Rothenberg, on a limited basis, of the status of the Special\n550: Committee's review of strategic alternatives and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and CSC and Pamplona to\n551: provide Moab with the opportunity to resume discussions with CSC/Pamplona regarding a possible equity rollover\n552: of Moab's shares in the transaction if Moab still had an interest in such a rollover. Mr. Rothenberg indicated\n553: he would like to discuss the possibility of an equity rollover with CSC/Pamplona and it was agreed that\n554: representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch would arrange and facilitate such a discussion.\n555: Later on September 23, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to\n556: receive an update on the negotiations from the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter regarding the\n557: CSC/Pamplona final proposal and request for exclusivity. Representatives from Goodwin Procter reviewed the\n558: terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal with the Special Committee, including the $21.25 per share cash\n559: consideration, the $15 million CSC reverse termination fee, certain post-signing covenants, the fact that\n560: there would be no financing contingency and that Pamplona was committing to fund 100% of the capital needed\n561: for closing. After discussion, the Special Committee authorized Mac-Gray to enter into an exclusivity\n562: agreement providing for exclusive negotiations until 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013 on the terms included in\n563: the CSC/Pamplona final proposal and authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter to continue\n564: negotiations with CSC/Pamplona based on guidance provided by the Special Committee at this meeting.\n565: On September 24, 2013, CSC, Pamplona and Mac-Gray executed the exclusivity agreement providing for exclusive\n566: negotiations until 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013, subject to certain conditions, including that CSC and\n567: Pamplona not adversely change or modify the terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal.\n568: On September 25, 2013, Goodwin Procter delivered to Kirkland & Ellis LLP, outside legal counsel to\n569: CSC/Pamplona, whom we refer to as Kirkland, the initial draft of the merger agreement. Between September 25,\n570: 2013 and October 7, 2013, the Transaction Committee and representatives from CSC/Pamplona, Goodwin Procter and\n571: Kirkland negotiated the terms of the merger agreement (including our ability to respond to unsolicited\n572: inquiries following the announcement of the transaction and our right to terminate the merger agreement to\n573: accept a superior proposal) and the Pamplona commitment letter. The parties discussed various open points in\n574: the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including the proposal by Mac-Gray that both Pamplona\n575: and CSC be responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, including liability for any and all\n576: damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain post-signing covenants and the parties'\n577: termination rights, including the amount of the termination fee payable by Mac-Gray to CSC under certain\n578: circumstances (which we refer to as the Mac-Gray termination fee), which Mac-Gray proposed should approximate\n579: 2.5% of the total equity value of the transaction. Also during this time, CSC and Pamplona were granted full\n580: access to the electronic data site and access to certain members of management and conducted confirmatory\n581: business and legal due diligence on Mac-Gray. Mr. Rothenberg engaged in various discussions with\n582: representatives of Pamplona during this time to discuss a possible rollover of Moab shares. As a result of\n583: such discussions, it was determined that Moab would not rollover any Moab shares in the transaction. Following\n584: such determination, BofA Merrill Lynch and the Transaction Committee provided Mr. Rothenberg with the details\n585: regarding the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and\n586: CSC/Pamplona.\n587: 38\n588: \n589: ========== PAGE 39 (segment 44, zeq=[38]) ==========\n590: Table of Contents\n591: Between September 24, 2013 and September 27, 2013, Kirkland and outside counsel to Mr. MacDonald negotiated\n592: the terms of the MacDonald voting agreements, which were entered into by Mr. MacDonald, his wife and one of\n593: his trusts on September 27, 2013, and which became effective upon entry into the merger agreement.\n594: On October 1, 2013, representatives of Mac-Gray management, along with representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch\n595: and representatives from CSC/Pamplona attended a meeting at the Boston offices of Goodwin Procter for purposes\n596: of conducting confirmatory business due diligence. Representatives of Mac-Gray, Goodwin Procter, BofA Merrill\n597: Lynch and representatives of CSC/Pamplona and Kirkland participated in various telephone conference calls over\n598: the subsequent days to finalize business and legal due diligence.\n599: On October 5, 2013, Kirkland delivered to Goodwin Procter a revised draft of the Pamplona commitment letter\n600: which, among other things, proposed that, in addition to CSC, Pamplona would be responsible in the event CSC\n601: failed to consummate the merger, but that Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the\n602: merger agreement would be limited to an unspecified dollar amount.\n603: On October 7, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive an\n604: update on the transaction negotiations from the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter. By invitation of\n605: the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald was also present for a portion of the meeting. Mr. Daoust reported to the\n606: Special Committee that, after discussions between Moab and representatives of Pamplona, it was determined that\n607: Moab would not rollover any Moab shares in the transaction. Mr. Daoust also provided the Special Committee\n608: with an overview of the open business points and the progress the parties had made since the September 23rd\n609: Special Committee meeting. Representatives from Goodwin Procter then reported on the progress that had been\n610: made in diligence and summarized the open issues in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter,\n611: including to what extent Pamplona would be responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger,\n612: including CSC's and Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain\n613: post-signing covenants and the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination\n614: fee and the CSC reverse termination fee. The Special Committee authorized the Transaction Committee and\n615: Goodwin Procter to continue negotiations with CSC/Pamplona, and, among other things, provided guidance with\n616: respect to the open transaction issues discussed at the meeting.\n617: Also on October 7, 2013, Kirkland delivered to Goodwin Procter a revised draft of the merger agreement which,\n618: among other things, proposed a $15 million Mac-Gray termination fee, which represented approximately 4.4% of\n619: the total equity value of the transaction, and certain positions on the scope of various post-signing\n620: covenants.\n621: On October 8, 2013, representatives from Goodwin Procter and Kirkland participated in a telephone conference\n622: call to discuss the open points in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including to what\n623: extent Pamplona would also have liability in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, which Kirkland\n624: proposed, at the direction of CSC/Pamplona, be capped at $50 million. Kirkland and Goodwin Procter also\n625: discussed CSC's and Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain\n626: post-signing covenants and the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination\n627: fee and the CSC reverse termination fee. The parties also discussed potential compromises on the post-signing\n628: covenants. Goodwin Procter and Kirkland agreed to communicate these positions to their respective clients.\n629: Later that day, after various discussions among representatives from CSC/Pamplona, Kirkland, Goodwin Procter\n630: and the members of the Transaction Committee, and on behalf of the Special Committee, Goodwin Procter sent an\n631: e-mail to the representatives of Kirkland that proposed to accept the\n632: 39\n633: \n634: ========== PAGE 40 (segment 45, zeq=[39]) ==========\n635: Table of Contents\n636: $50 million liability cap proposed by Pamplona and proposed a $10.5 million Mac-Gray termination fee, which\n637: represented approximately 3.0% of the total equity value of the transaction.\n638: On October 9, 2013, in follow up to the October 8, 2013 negotiations and proposals made by Goodwin Procter,\n639: the members of the Transaction Committee participated in a telephone conference call with a representative\n640: from CSC/Pamplona to discuss the open issues in the merger agreement, including certain post-signing covenants\n641: and the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the CSC reverse\n642: termination fee, but did not reach agreement on these issues.\n643: Later on October 9, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive\n644: an update on the transaction negotiations from the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter. By invitation of\n645: the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald was also present at this meeting. The Transaction Committee reported to\n646: the Special Committee on the negotiations that had taken place since the meeting of the Special Committee held\n647: on October 7, 2013. The Transaction Committee reported that the parties had come to an agreement that\n648: Pamplona's